window.AppNhoPhase15EN = {"lessons": {"1": {"title": "The Role of a Textile Testing Laboratory", "cat": "Laboratory Systems", "body": ["A textile laboratory verifies the properties of raw materials, semi-finished materials and finished products against technical requirements. Test results support sample approval, incoming-material control, defect investigations and change validation; they do not replace process control on the production line.", "Distinguish development testing, routine quality-control testing and testing for certification or compliance. Each purpose has different requirements for methods, personnel competence, equipment, traceability and independence.", "Before testing, define the question to be answered, the standard or method and its revision, the representative sample, sample quantity, preconditioning requirements and acceptance criteria. Do not select a test merely because the laboratory already has the equipment.", "Technical basis. A laboratory provides measurement evidence for raw materials, semi-finished materials and finished products. Results may support material approval, substantiation of product claims, lot control, complaint investigations and change assessment. A laboratory does not replace production-line control: a sample-based test describes only the portion tested under the stated conditions.", "Implementation sequence and parameters to lock. Define the service scope, test catalogue, methods, equipment capability and personnel competence. Each request should specify the technical question, sample ID, method and revision, conditioning requirements, criteria, due date and recipient. Distinguish in-house process-control testing, buyer-facing testing and tests that must be outsourced to a suitably capable laboratory.", "Interpreting results and handling deviations. A passing report does not automatically prove that an entire lot conforms if the sampling plan is not representative. When results support a claim or compliance statement, verify the relevant accreditation scope, method and conditions of application."], "deep": "Before accepting a request, the laboratory must define the sample scope, test, method, equipment and personnel competence. Distinguish in-house process-control tests from results used for customer approval or conformity claims. Each result must be traceable to the sample, method revision, operator, equipment and environmental conditions. If a test is outside the laboratory's capability, refer it to a suitably capable laboratory and state the limits of the service clearly.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The Role of a Textile Testing Laboratory\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>The Role of a Textile Testing Laboratory</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">The Role of a Textile Testing Laboratory</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Request</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Lab scope</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Method</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Report</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "2": {"title": "Building a Test Catalogue and Requirements Matrix", "cat": "Laboratory Systems", "body": ["A test matrix links product type, fibre composition, fabric construction, market, buyer and use-related risks to the tests required. For example, sportswear may require checks for colourfastness, dimensional stability, air permeability, moisture management and seam strength.", "Not every product requires every test. Use risk assessment to identify mandatory tests, periodic tests, tests triggered by a change and tests used to investigate a defect.", "Control the matrix by revision. When a buyer changes a standard, a market regulation changes or a supplier changes a material, assess the impact before updating the test plan.", "Technical basis. Organise the test catalogue by material, product, risk and claim rather than simply by the equipment available. The requirements matrix connects customer or legal requirements to the test, method, sample, acceptance criteria, frequency and responsibility. Separate mandatory requirements, buyer requirements and internal tests used for risk management.", "Implementation sequence and parameters to lock. Start from the controlled versions of the specification and bill of materials, then break each requirement into a measurable property. Select a suitable method and define sample condition, quantity, wash or ageing cycles, laboratory and required result date. Flag tests outside internal capability, outsourced work and hold points before production.", "Interpreting results and handling deviations. Do not copy the matrix from another style if fibre composition, construction, market or product claim differs. When a specification changes, update the matrix and assess the impact on existing reports."], "deep": "Build a matrix for each style and requirement, covering the property, method, acceptance criterion, test stage, frequency, required sample and approving party. Separate legal or market requirements, buyer requirements and internal standards; record the source and revision. Identify tests required before bulk, periodic tests and tests triggered only by a material change. Update the matrix when the market, composition, colour, finish or product claim changes.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Building a Test Catalogue and Requirements Matrix\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Building a Test Catalogue and Requirements Matrix</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Building a Test Catalogue and Requirements Matrix</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Style / product code</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Property</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Criteria</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "3": {"title": "Test Standards: ISO, AATCC, ASTM and Buyer Methods", "cat": "Laboratory Systems", "body": ["ISO, AATCC, ASTM and buyer-specific methods may measure the same property while differing in specimen preparation, equipment, cycles, evaluation and reporting. Similar test names do not mean that results are directly interchangeable.", "The test request must state the full method code, year or revision, selected options, number of cycles and acceptance criteria. If a contract says only “colourfastness test”, clarify the exact method before testing.", "Do not copy acceptance limits from one customer to another. A test standard generally describes how to perform the test; the pass/fail requirement may be stated separately in a specification or contract.", "Technical basis and test scope. ISO, AATCC and ASTM publish methods or standards within different scopes; a buyer may add an internal method, special conditions or a specific evaluation procedure. The organisation's name alone does not identify the test. Record the complete method code, applicable year or revision, relevant appendices and any contractually approved modifications.", "Preparation, execution and required records. When a request arrives, prepare a comparison table covering the property, method code, revision, specimen condition, equipment, conditions, sample quantity, calculation and reporting rule. If two documents conflict, do not choose the easier requirement; raise a technical query with the buyer or specification owner to confirm precedence. Check the controlled edition applicable on the test date rather than relying on an old saved copy.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Never infer an acceptance limit from the method name. A method describes the test procedure; acceptance criteria usually come from a specification, regulation, contract or product document. Keep “test method” separate from “acceptance requirement” in the records to avoid confusion when standards are revised."], "deep": "ISO, AATCC, ASTM and buyer methods may differ in specimen preparation, equipment, conditions, sample quantity and result calculation. Record the complete method code, year or revision, permitted options and every approved deviation. Do not report only “tested to ASTM” without identifying the number and revision. When two methods produce different results, examine differences in test conditions before concluding that the material is unstable.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Test Standards: ISO, AATCC, ASTM and Buyer Methods\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Test Standards: ISO, AATCC, ASTM and Buyer Methods</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Test Standards: ISO, AATCC, ASTM and Buyer Methods</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Standard</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Revision</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Deviation</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Approval</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "4": {"title": "Representative Sampling and Sampling Plans", "cat": "Sampling and Specimen Preparation", "body": ["Samples must represent the lot being evaluated. Define the lot unit, number of rolls, cartons or products, sampling locations, random-selection method, retained samples and the procedure for handling a non-uniform lot.", "Do not take samples only from good-looking rolls or convenient locations. For shade, shrinkage or lengthwise-defect risks, sample multiple rolls and locations; record the roll ID, metre position, fabric face and direction.", "Agree on the sampling plan before results are known. If the sample count is increased after an unfavourable result, record it as an additional investigation; do not present it as part of the original plan.", "Technical basis and test scope. A test result supports conclusions only within the scope allowed by the sampling plan. A sample from one roll, colour or location does not automatically represent a lot containing multiple suppliers, production shifts or finishing conditions. The plan must define the lot, sampling unit, selection method, quantity, locations and decision purpose.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Define the sampling unit (roll, fabric length, carton or garment), lot definition, random or stratified selection method, sample quantity and rules for replacing damaged specimens. For roll goods, consider the beginning and end of rolls, selvedge-to-centre variation and different dye lots. For finished garments, stratify by size, colour, line or production time when these factors may affect the result. Record every sample that could not be collected and the reason.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Do not convert an attribute-inspection plan into a continuous-measurement plan without evaluating the statistical method. AQL, inspection level and sample size must follow a buyer-approved or quality-system plan; do not invent one universal number for every lot. If the sample is not representative, limit the conclusion and request additional sampling."], "deep": "Sampling must represent the lot and the purpose of inspection. Define the lot unit, number of rolls, cartons or products, sampling locations and random or stratified selection method. Sample from the beginning, middle and end of a roll, or from multiple width positions, when lengthwise or cross-width variation is a risk. Avoid selecting only visually good samples. Record the lot code, roll number, location, date and time, sampler and seal condition so the result can be traced.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Representative Sampling and Sampling Plans\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Representative Sampling and Sampling Plans</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Representative Sampling and Sampling Plans</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Define lot</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Select unit</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Collect sample</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Seal</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "5": {"title": "Specimen Cutting, Direction Marking and Bias Prevention", "cat": "Sampling and Specimen Preparation", "body": ["Before cutting, identify the warp and weft or wale and course directions, face side, pile or nap direction, specimen length and any areas that must be excluded, such as selvedges or local defect zones, where required by the method.", "Use sharp cutting tools and avoid stretching, creasing, heating or contaminating the specimen. Mark it by a method that does not affect the test area; do not use a pen or ink that may migrate into the measurement zone.", "Keep a cutting layout so that the position of every specimen can be identified. When testing multiple directions, record each direction separately rather than combining the results.", "Technical basis and test scope. Fabric is anisotropic: properties in the lengthwise, crosswise or bias direction may differ because of yarn structure, density, twist, weave or knit direction and finishing. Incorrect cutting direction can make tensile, tear, stretch or shrinkage results unrepresentative of the property being assessed. Cutting location also matters because selvedges, roll ends and defect areas may differ from the main fabric body.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Before cutting, lay the fabric flat and identify the face, lengthwise and crosswise directions, pile or nap, pattern direction and selvedge position. Use a suitable cutting template, sharp blade and clean table; avoid stretching, creasing or heating the specimen edge. Mark the sample ID and direction without affecting the test area. If the method requires specimens in a specific direction, prepare the required number for each direction and test.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Do not treat several adjacent specimens as independent samples when the purpose is to assess variation across the width or lot. Retain the cutting layout with the report so that the original positions can be traced. If a specimen is skewed, frayed or clamp-damaged, reject it under the defined rule and record its replacement."], "deep": "When cutting specimens, identify the lengthwise and crosswise directions, face and reverse, selvedge and centre positions, and dimensions required by the method. Use a suitable template and avoid stretching the fabric or fraying the edges. Mark specimens in a way that does not alter their colour or chemistry; do not use markers that can affect the measurement area. Arrange specimens by test code, direction and replicate number. If a specimen is cut in the wrong direction, discard and record it rather than relabelling it.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Specimen Cutting, Direction Marking and Bias Prevention\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Specimen Cutting, Direction Marking and Bias Prevention</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Specimen Cutting, Direction Marking and Bias Prevention</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Specimen direction</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Cut specimen</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Mark</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Group</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "6": {"title": "Conditioning Specimens in a Standard Atmosphere", "cat": "Sampling and Specimen Preparation", "body": ["Many textile tests require specimens to be conditioned in a specified atmosphere before measurement. Temperature, relative humidity, conditioning time and equilibrium state can affect mass, dimensions, strength and static charge.", "Do not treat one temperature and humidity value as the universal condition for every test. Follow the applicable standard and record actual conditions using appropriately calibrated monitoring equipment.", "A specimen recently taken from a damp store, dried or laundered may not yet be in equilibrium. Record when it enters the conditioning room and when it is tested; do not shorten the conditioning period merely to meet a schedule.", "Technical basis and test scope. Humidity and temperature can change the mass, dimensions, strength, elongation and feel of textile materials. Hygroscopic fibres such as cotton and viscose respond differently from polyester; fabric construction, coatings and processing history also affect moisture-equilibration time. Conditioning is therefore part of the test method, not an arbitrary waiting step.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Record the environmental conditions required by the applicable method, the time the specimen enters the room, how it is hung or laid out, its distance from air sources and the criterion used to determine that conditioning is complete. Do not stack specimens densely or place them against walls or air outlets where conditions may be uneven. For multilayer or coated materials, confirm the conditioning period against the method and specimen characteristics.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. If the room conditions fall outside the controlled limits, pause testing or record a deviation; do not report the result as though it were obtained under standard conditions. The report should state actual conditions and how the deviation was handled. Comparisons across days must consider the conditioning history, not only the final measurement."], "deep": "Conditioning brings a material to a stable moisture and temperature state before tests that are sensitive to the environment. Record temperature, relative humidity, conditioning time, specimen location and the time it is removed for testing. Thick, multilayer or highly absorbent materials may require different equilibration periods under the applicable method. Do not leave a conditioned specimen outside the room for an extended period before testing. Record environmental deviations so their effect on the result can be assessed.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Conditioning Specimens in a Standard Atmosphere\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Conditioning Specimens in a Standard Atmosphere</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Conditioning Specimens in a Standard Atmosphere</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Atmosphere</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Conditioning</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Time</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Testing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "7": {"title": "Measurement Uncertainty and Repeatability", "cat": "Laboratory Systems", "body": ["Test results are affected by equipment, operators, the environment, the specimen and the method. Repeatability describes how close results are under similar conditions; reproducibility considers more substantial changes, such as a different laboratory or operator.", "Measurement uncertainty helps interpret the range of doubt associated with a result. When a result is close to a limit, the decision rule and treatment of borderline results under the contract or method must be understood.", "Do not round values arbitrarily to turn a failure into a pass. Reports must retain resolution appropriate to the method and state the rounding rule where one applies.", "Technical basis and test scope. Repeatability describes the closeness of results when the same measurement conditions are maintained; reproducibility considers larger changes, such as different operators, equipment or laboratories. Measurement uncertainty expresses the dispersion reasonably associated with a result. These concepts are related but are not interchangeable, and cannot be assessed merely by repeating a measurement a few times and taking the average.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Identify contributing sources: instrument resolution, calibration, contact force or pressure, specimen location, environmental conditions, operator technique, specimen preparation and inherent material variation. Design repeated measurements to suit the purpose; separate within-specimen variation from between-specimen variation. Record raw data, the number of measurements, the treatment of outliers and the calculation method under the approved procedure.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. When a result is close to an acceptance limit, consider uncertainty and the agreed decision rule. Do not subtract or add uncertainty to a result on your own to create a passing status. If dispersion is abnormal, investigate the method and measurement system before concluding that the material is non-uniform."], "deep": "Measurement uncertainty describes the doubt associated with a result, while repeatability describes the closeness of results under the same conditions. Distinguish repeatability within the same laboratory, operator and equipment from reproducibility across different conditions. Sources of variation include sampling, preparation, equipment, operator, environment and material heterogeneity. When a result is close to a limit, apply the agreed uncertainty and decision rule; do not round the value to change its pass/fail status.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Measurement Uncertainty and Repeatability\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Measurement Uncertainty and Repeatability</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Measurement Uncertainty and Repeatability</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Sources of variation</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Repeatability</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Uncertainty</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Decision</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "8": {"title": "Equipment Calibration, Verification and Maintenance", "cat": "Equipment Management", "body": ["Calibration establishes the relationship between an instrument indication and a measurement standard; verification confirms that the equipment meets its intended-use criteria. These activities are not the same, and their frequency should reflect risk, use, drift history and accreditation requirements.", "Equipment needs an identification code, calibration status, measurement range, resolution, accessories, software, instructions and maintenance log. Overdue equipment or equipment showing abnormal behaviour must be taken out of service.", "When equipment is found to be out of tolerance, retrospectively assess results issued since the most recent successful verification. Do not simply repair the equipment and resume testing while ignoring the possible effect on lots already tested.", "Technical basis and test scope. Calibration establishes the relationship between an instrument indication and a traceable measurement standard; verification checks whether the equipment meets its intended-use requirements; maintenance keeps it operational. These activities have different purposes. A current calibration label does not by itself prove that the instrument is suitable for every test.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Maintain an equipment register covering asset ID, measurement range, resolution, method requirements, calibration and verification schedule, status, responsible person and repair history. Before a test shift, inspect the equipment, check zero, reference standards or control specimens where required by the method. If equipment is overdue, dropped or produces an abnormal verification result, lock it out and assess the impact on results issued since the last successful check.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Do not adjust equipment to make a result look “better” without an approved procedure and before-and-after records. After repair or relocation, reassess the equipment before returning it to service. Records must link each test report to the exact equipment and its status on the test date."], "deep": "Calibration links an instrument indication to a measurement standard; verification checks whether equipment meets its intended-use requirements; maintenance keeps it operating reliably. Maintain an equipment register with ID, location, range, status, calibration and verification schedule, and responsible person. After repair, relocation or impact, assess the equipment before using it again. A current calibration label does not replace daily functional checks.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Equipment Calibration, Verification and Maintenance\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Equipment Calibration, Verification and Maintenance</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Equipment Calibration, Verification and Maintenance</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Equipment</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Calibration</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Verification</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Maintenance</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "9": {"title": "Tester Competence and Control of Test Operations", "cat": "Equipment Management", "body": ["Testers must be trained in the specific method, not merely shown how to switch on a machine. Competence includes specimen preparation, setup, fault recognition, result reading, data recording and understanding the limits of the test.", "Use reference specimens, internal proficiency checks, observed practice and comparisons between testers. For visual assessments, control lighting, background, viewing angle and assessor capability.", "Define report-approval authority clearly. The person performing the test, the reviewer and the approver must be recorded so that responsibility can be traced in a dispute.", "Technical basis and test scope. Test results depend on operator competence at sample identification, preparation, cutting, mounting, equipment setup, endpoint observation and data recording. Even a detailed SOP can produce different results if critical actions are not standardised or the tester's competence has not been assessed.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Define competence for each test, not merely by job title. Training records should include content, SOP revision, supervised practice, reference or control specimens, performance assessment and approver. When a method or equipment changes, reassess the affected competence. Blind trials or inter-operator comparisons can help detect differences in technique.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Years of experience alone are not sufficient evidence. If a tester's results show a systematic bias, examine specimen placement, applied force, scale reading, recording time and outlier handling. Assign tests only to authorised competent personnel and retain the authorisation list for each method."], "deep": "Tester competence must be demonstrated through training, observed practice, reference specimens, result comparisons and periodic assessment. For tests involving visual evaluation, standardise lighting, rating scales and reference samples. Record both the tester and reviewer on the report. When a method or instrument changes, reconfirm competence before assigning independent work. Analyse differences between testers to identify training needs.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Tester Competence and Control of Test Operations\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Tester Competence and Control of Test Operations</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Tester Competence and Control of Test Operations</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Training</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Execution</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assessment</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Authorisation</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "10": {"title": "Management of Chemicals, Reagents and Standard Solutions", "cat": "Equipment Management", "body": ["Chemicals and reagents must be identified and labelled with concentration, opening date, expiry date, storage conditions and the relevant SDS. Prepared solutions must record the preparer, method-based formulation, preparation date and period of validity.", "Do not replace reagents or detergents specified in a standard method with a seemingly similar commercial product. Active ingredients, pH and additives can change the test result.", "Establish controls for water quality, glassware cleanliness, cross-contamination and chemical waste. When a supplier changes, assess equivalence before use.", "Technical basis and test scope. The reliability of chemical tests depends on chemical identity, purity, concentration, expiry and storage conditions. Contaminated, evaporated or degraded reagents can change the reaction and result even when other operations remain unchanged. Standard solutions need preparation records, traceability to the source material and a validity period defined by procedure.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Control chemicals from receipt: check labels, batch numbers, certificates of analysis where available, SDS, package condition and transport conditions. When preparing a solution, record mass or volume, equipment, preparer, preparation date, concentration calculation and solution ID. Store chemicals by compatibility group; control temperature, light and ventilation according to the manufacturer's instructions.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Do not use an unlabelled bottle or expired solution merely because its appearance seems normal. Verify standard solutions with a control sample or an appropriate standardisation procedure before use. When a deviation is found, identify tests that used the affected chemical or batch and assess whether retesting may be necessary."], "deep": "Chemicals require an identification label, concentration, preparation or opening date, expiry, storage conditions and a responsible person. Standard solutions must be traceable to certificates, batch numbers, uncertainty information and storage conditions. Do not use expired or unidentified chemicals merely because their appearance has not changed. When preparing a solution, record mass or volume, equipment, dilution factor and checker. Segregate incompatible substances and control waste after use.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Management of Chemicals, Reagents and Standard Solutions\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Management of Chemicals, Reagents and Standard Solutions</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Management of Chemicals, Reagents and Standard Solutions</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Chemicals</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Standard solution</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Preparation</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Records</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "11": {"title": "GSM and Mass per Unit Area Measurement", "cat": "Physical Testing", "body": ["GSM is the mass per square metre. The result depends on sampling, specimen area, balance accuracy, specimen conditioning and whether the specimen has reached moisture equilibrium.", "For a specimen cut to a known area, calculate GSM by dividing its mass by its area, using compatible units. If a circular cutter is used, verify the actual cutting area, blade condition and the method for taking samples from different positions across the fabric width.", "Report the mean, variation and sampling locations where required. An acceptable average GSM can still conceal substantial variation between the selvedge and the centre of the fabric.", "Technical basis and test scope. GSM is mass per unit area, commonly expressed in g/m². The result depends on specimen mass, cutting area, moisture content, coatings and fabric uniformity. For patterned, pile or cross-width-variable materials, one specimen may not represent the full fabric width.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Check the cutting template and balance before measurement; confirm the actual specimen area required by the method. Weigh the specimen after conditioning, record the reading and calculate GSM by dividing mass by area with consistent unit conversion. When several specimens are used, report individual values, the mean and variation as required. Mark sampling positions across the fabric width so edge-to-centre variation can be assessed.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. For example, a specimen with an area of 100 cm² and a mass of 1.8 g is equivalent to 180 g/m²; this conversion illustrates the calculation and is not an acceptance criterion. If GSM is outside expectation, check moisture, template area, finishing add-on, construction and sampling location before attributing the result to yarn linear density."], "deep": "Calculate GSM from specimen mass divided by specimen area, with consistent unit conversion. Results are affected by sampling position, moisture, fabric width and material non-uniformity. Use a cutter with a defined area, a balance suitable for the measurement range and a zero check before weighing. Take multiple specimens distributed across the width and length according to the plan. Report the mean, variation and sampling locations; a single specimen does not represent an entire fabric roll.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"GSM and Mass per Unit Area Measurement\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>GSM and Mass per Unit Area Measurement</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">GSM and Mass per Unit Area Measurement</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Specimen area</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Weigh</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Calculate GSM</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Report</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "12": {"title": "Fabric Thickness and Compressibility Measurement", "cat": "Physical Testing", "body": ["Fabric thickness depends on presser-foot pressure, contact area, dwell time, surface flatness and construction. Pile fabrics, fleece, foam laminates and other compressible materials are particularly sensitive to measurement conditions.", "Do not compare readings from two instruments with different pressure or presser-foot configurations unless equivalence has been demonstrated. Record the method, measuring load and number of measurement points.", "Measure several representative locations. Avoid folds, seams or local defects when the objective is to determine base-material thickness. If a local defect is being investigated, record its location separately.", "Technical basis and test scope. Fabric thickness is a measurement affected by applied pressure, presser-foot area, contact time and surface condition. Open knitted structures, fleece, spacer fabrics and pile materials are compressible; results cannot be compared when measurement conditions differ. Unloaded thickness and thickness under pressure are different states.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Select the presser foot and pressure specified by the method; check surface flatness, instrument zero and stabilisation time. Place the specimen without tension and avoid folds, seams or local defects unless they are the subject of the test. Measure multiple distributed locations and record the load and contact area. For compression testing, retain the load–deformation curve or the specified load points.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. When a material recovers slowly, the interval between measurements can affect the result. Do not measure the same point repeatedly before the specimen has recovered. Interpret thickness together with construction, porosity and intended use; thickness alone does not establish thermal insulation or durability."], "deep": "Thickness depends on measuring-foot pressure, contact area, dwell time and compressible construction. Record the method and instrument so results can be compared across lots. Measure multiple points, avoiding folds, defective edges or abnormal areas when a representative value is required; record local defect positions separately when investigating a defect. For foam, laminates or quilted materials, measure individual layers and the assembled structure where necessary. Do not compare readings taken at different pressure levels as though they represented the same measurand.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Fabric Thickness and Compressibility Measurement\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Fabric Thickness and Compressibility Measurement</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Fabric Thickness and Compressibility Measurement</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Measuring pressure</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Location</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Thickness</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Compare</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "13": {"title": "Usable Fabric Width and Width Variation", "cat": "Physical Testing", "body": ["Overall width and usable width may differ because of selvedges, needle holes, unusable zones or marker requirements. Define the measurement reference points and fabric condition before recording a result.", "Measure after conditioning and lay the fabric flat using a consistent method; do not stretch it to achieve the required width. For roll goods, check several positions along the length to identify width variation.", "Compare actual usable width with the width assumed in the marker and consumption calculation. Width variation can reduce cutting efficiency and cause fabric shortages even when GSM is within specification.", "Technical basis and test scope. Usable width is the portion of the fabric width available for production under the applicable requirements; it is not necessarily the full edge-to-edge width. Defective selvedges, roll edges, creased zones or areas with unacceptable shade can reduce the practical cutting width. Width variation directly affects marker layout, material consumption and the placement of pattern pieces.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Define how selvedges and exclusion zones are identified under the specification. Measure at multiple positions along the roll, keeping the tape or rule perpendicular to the edges and avoiding fabric tension. Record overall width, usable width, measurement position, roll length and the type of defect affecting usable width. For variable rolls, report the minimum and maximum or the distribution required by the agreed control plan.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. If usable width is reduced, recalculate the impact on marker efficiency and consumption before acceptance. Do not rely only on the average if a short section below the required width could disrupt the cutting layout. Retain the defect map and roll-level measurements so the issue can be traced to the supplier or finishing operation."], "deep": "Usable width is the portion of fabric that can be used in production after excluding selvedges, defects or other areas under the marker rules. Measure at several positions along the roll after the fabric has stabilised. Record overall and usable width separately, along with defective-edge locations, edge distortion and changes along the roll. When width varies, assess the effect on marker layout, consumption and size placement; one measurement point cannot represent the entire lot.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Usable Fabric Width and Width Variation\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Usable Fabric Width and Width Variation</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Usable Fabric Width and Width Variation</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Overall width</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Defective selvedge</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Usable width</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Marker</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "14": {"title": "Tensile Strength and Elongation Testing", "cat": "Mechanical Testing", "body": ["Tensile testing measures a specimen's response to a tensile load under a defined configuration. Results depend on specimen type, width, gauge length, test speed, fabric direction and preconditioning.", "Distinguish breaking force, elongation at break and properties measured at a specified load. Do not compare grab-test results with strip-test results without accounting for the differences between the methods.", "Observe the failure mode, slippage from the grips, breaks at the grips and specimen-preparation defects. Abnormal results caused by handling must be investigated; they must not be excluded without an established rule.", "Technical basis and test scope. Tensile testing determines the material response to a tensile load applied in a specified direction and specimen geometry. Results may include maximum force, force at a defined extension, elongation at break or the load–extension curve. Results cannot be directly compared when specimen width, gauge length, test speed or specimen direction differs.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Prepare specimens in the lengthwise and crosswise directions, avoiding frayed cut edges and defects outside the scope of the test. Align the specimen in the grips without slack or unapproved pre-load. Record grip type, gauge length, speed, conditioning, failure mode and break location. Report each direction separately and state how slippage or grip breaks are handled.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. The load–extension curve helps distinguish brittle materials, materials with substantial deformation and constructions that redistribute load. If force drops unexpectedly, check grip slippage, specimen-width variation, cut-edge damage and load-cell calibration. Do not report only maximum force when the product requirement concerns elongation at a service load."], "deep": "Tensile testing requires a defined method, specimen direction and width, test speed, grip separation and clamping arrangement. Record breaking force, elongation or force at the specified extension according to the requirement. Handle grip breaks and specimen slippage under the method's rules. Analyse lengthwise and crosswise results and failure modes separately; a fabric test does not replace seam or finished-garment evaluation. Compare results only when specimens and conditions are equivalent.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Tensile Strength and Elongation Testing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Tensile Strength and Elongation Testing</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Tensile Strength and Elongation Testing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Specimen direction</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Grip specimen</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Apply tension</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Failure mode</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "15": {"title": "Tear Strength Testing", "cat": "Mechanical Testing", "body": ["Tear strength reflects a material's resistance to the propagation of a tear under a defined method. Fabric construction, density, filament characteristics, finishing and test direction strongly affect the result.", "Elmendorf, trouser and other tear methods use different principles and specimen geometries. Do not apply one universal limit or change the method without approval.", "Record warp and weft directions, failure mode, variation and invalid specimens. When results are low, examine yarn structure, density, enzyme or finishing treatment and damage caused during cutting.", "Technical basis and test scope. Tear strength describes a material's resistance to the propagation of an existing tear. The tearing mechanism differs from breaking an intact specimen in tension; weave or knit structure, yarn density, interlacement, tear direction and specimen geometry strongly affect the result. The method and specimen geometry must therefore be identified.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Cut specimens to the required dimensions and make the prescribed slit using a template; do not allow the slit to become extended or misaligned. Mount the specimen according to the grip arrangement and check the load and test speed. Record force according to the method, tear-propagation pattern and any yarn pull-out, breakage or slippage. If the method requires graph processing, retain raw data and the rule used to determine the reported value.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. An unusually low result may be caused by an incorrect slit, yarn damage during cutting, wrong test direction or non-uniform construction. Compare materials only under the same method and conditions. Do not infer tear strength from tensile strength; the two tests describe different failure mechanisms."], "deep": "Tear testing evaluates tear propagation under a specific specimen configuration and method; it cannot be replaced by tensile testing. Select the correct specimen type, direction, slit preparation and force calculation under the applicable standard. Fabric construction, density, yarn characteristics and finishing strongly affect the tear path. Record failure mode and variation between specimens. For coated or laminated fabrics, identify which layer tears or delaminates first.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Tear Strength Testing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Tear Strength Testing</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Tear Strength Testing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Make slit</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Tear</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Tear path</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Result</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "16": {"title": "Seam Strength and Seam Slippage Testing", "cat": "Mechanical Testing", "body": ["Seam strength evaluates the load-bearing capacity of a sewn seam; seam slippage evaluates yarn displacement at or near the seam under a defined loading configuration. The properties are related but not identical.", "Results depend on seam type, stitch type, stitches per inch, thread, needle, seam allowance, fabric direction and the conditions used to sew the test specimen. The test specimen must represent the production configuration.", "When a specimen fails, examine both the fabric and sewing parameters: stitch density, thread tension, thread type, needle type, distance from the edge and seam construction. Do not attribute the failure entirely to the fabric before examining the sewn specimen.", "Technical basis and test scope. Seam strength evaluates the ability of a sewn assembly to withstand load; seam slippage evaluates yarn displacement or separation near the seam. Both may occur together, but they are not equivalent. Results are affected by the fabric, seam type, stitch density, thread, needle, distance from the seam to the edge and loading direction.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Prepare specimens using the locked production sewing configuration and record the machine, needle, thread, stitch density, seam type and operator. Position the seam correctly in the grips, apply load in the specified direction and observe the failure mode: thread breakage, fabric rupture, skipped stitches, yarn slippage or seam opening. Report force together with the failure mode; a single force value does not explain the mechanism.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. If the fabric fails while the thread remains intact, examine fabric construction and seam position. If the thread breaks, check thread, needle, tension and stitch density. Specimens must represent the actual sewing process; hand-sewn samples made with a different configuration do not provide sufficient evidence for bulk approval."], "deep": "Seam strength and seam slippage are related but distinct: a seam may fail through thread or fabric breakage, while seam slippage is yarn displacement at the seam. Select seam type, thread, needle, stitch density and specimen configuration to match the product. Record force, seam opening and failure mode. Results vary with seam direction and distance from the edge. For load-bearing products, test actual constructions at areas such as underarms, crotch seams, pockets or joining points.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Seam Strength and Seam Slippage Testing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Seam Strength and Seam Slippage Testing</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Seam Strength and Seam Slippage Testing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Sewing configuration</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Applied load</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Seam slippage</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Failure mode</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "17": {"title": "Abrasion Resistance Testing", "cat": "Mechanical Testing", "body": ["Abrasion testing evaluates resistance to repeated friction. Different instruments and endpoints may be used, including yarn breakage, appearance change or mass loss; results depend on the method.", "Martindale and other methods are not automatically equivalent. Record the abrasive material, load, motion, number of cycles and endpoint criterion required by the applicable method revision.", "Assess specimens under consistent observation conditions. For products with different wear zones, such as elbows, knees or trouser seats, select test locations and materials according to actual use risks.", "Technical basis and test scope. Abrasion is surface deterioration caused by contact and relative movement. Mechanisms may include fibre pull-out, yarn breakage, surface polishing, coating wear or structural thinning. Results depend on the instrument, abrasive material, load, motion, endpoint and damage-assessment method.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Define the test method and endpoint before testing: a specified number of cycles, a damage level or a change in mass or appearance. Prepare flat, wrinkle-free specimens and secure the specified load and abrasive material. Record cycle count, damage morphology, photographs and assessor. For visual assessment, use a controlled rating scale and agreed reference specimens.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Do not compare cycle counts from different methods. If damage is concentrated in one area, check load distribution and specimen mounting. For coated or laminated fabrics, distinguish surface wear from layer separation; these failure modes have different causes and corrective actions."], "deep": "Record the instrument, abrasive material, load, cycle conditions, endpoint and assessment method for abrasion testing. A method may measure cycles to failure or evaluate surface change after a defined number of cycles. Do not compare results directly across different instruments or abrasive heads. For coated fabrics, inspect substrate exposure, coating cracks and delamination. Select test locations according to product friction mechanisms, such as elbows, knees or backpack contact areas.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Abrasion Resistance Testing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Abrasion Resistance Testing</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Abrasion Resistance Testing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Abrasion instrument</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Load / cycles</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Endpoint</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assessment</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "18": {"title": "Evaluation of Pilling, Fuzzing and Snagging", "cat": "Mechanical Testing", "body": ["Pilling is the formation of fibre balls on a surface; fuzzing is the formation of surface fibres or hairiness; snagging occurs when yarns are caught and pulled. These phenomena may occur together but require separate assessment criteria.", "Equipment, cycles, load, reference fabric and rating scale affect the result. Image-based assessment must follow the specified scale and lighting; do not change the rating scale between test rounds.", "For investigations, examine fibre structure, staple length, twist, filament yarns, brushing, enzyme treatment, resin and friction during use. An anti-pilling finish may improve appearance while affecting hand feel or strength.", "Technical basis and test scope. Fuzzing is the formation of raised fibres on the surface; pilling occurs when fibres become entangled into pills; snagging is the pulling of yarns out of the structure after they catch on an object. The phenomena are related, but one assessment method cannot replace another. Fibre type, yarn structure, twist, fabric density, finishing and use conditions all have an influence.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Select a friction or snagging method suited to the purpose. Prepare specimens in the required direction, condition them and mount them correctly. After the specified cycles or duration, assess the specimens under stable lighting and background conditions using a controlled rating scale. Use reference images or physical standards to reduce differences between assessors.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. When ratings vary between observers, conduct a reference-specimen alignment and visual-assessment calibration exercise. Record fabric face, location, cycle count and defect type. Do not combine pilling and snagging into one overall score when the specification requires separate results."], "deep": "Pilling is the formation of fibre balls on the surface; fuzzing is raised surface fibre; snagging is yarn pulled out of the structure after catching. Use a separate method and rating scale appropriate to each fabric type. Observe under consistent lighting, background and viewing angle, using approved reference images or specimens. Record cycles, equipment and specimen location. Knitted fabrics and filament-yarn fabrics may have a different snagging risk from staple-fibre woven fabrics.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Evaluation of Pilling, Fuzzing and Snagging\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Evaluation of Pilling, Fuzzing and Snagging</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Evaluation of Pilling, Fuzzing and Snagging</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Pilling</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Fuzzing</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Snagging</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Classification</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "19": {"title": "Colourfastness to Laundering", "cat": "Colourfastness Testing", "body": ["Colourfastness to laundering evaluates colour change in the specimen and staining on adjacent material under a specified washing cycle. Results depend on temperature, detergent, load, steel balls where applicable, time and number of cycles.", "Distinguish colour change in the specimen from staining on the multifibre adjacent fabric. Record the two results separately; do not combine them into one score when the method requires separate reporting.", "Inspect specimens before and after testing under the correct lighting and rating scale. When a specimen fails, investigate dye type, fixation, washing-off, finishing and the risk of colour migration.", "Technical basis and test scope. The laundering test evaluates colour change in the specimen and transfer of colour to adjacent material under specified washing conditions. Results are affected by temperature, washing chemicals, time, liquor ratio, mechanical action, number of cycles and drying method. One test does not represent every care programme.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Confirm the method, detergent type, adjacent fabric, washing conditions and post-wash procedure. Prepare the specimen and adjacent fabric to the required dimensions and sew or attach them as instructed; place them in the apparatus with the appropriate load. After testing, treat the specimens as specified and assess colour change and staining separately using the required grey scale or instrument.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Report both results and the test conditions. A specimen that changes colour without staining adjacent fabric may have a different cause from one that transfers colour. If results fail, examine the dye, washing-off process, pH and post-dyeing treatment before proposing process changes."], "deep": "For laundering fastness, define the wash cycle, detergent, temperature, load, adjacent fabric and drying procedure under the method. Assess specimen colour change and staining on adjacent fabric separately. For multicolour specimens, record the assessment areas and colours at risk of bleeding. Retain before-and-after photographs under consistent observation conditions. Passing one cycle does not automatically demonstrate suitability for every care label or number of washes.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Colourfastness to Laundering\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Colourfastness to Laundering</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Colourfastness to Laundering</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Laundering</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Adjacent fabric</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Colour change</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Staining</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "20": {"title": "Dry and Wet Rubbing Colourfastness", "cat": "Colourfastness Testing", "body": ["Rubbing or crocking evaluates the amount of colour transferred from a specimen surface to a standard rubbing cloth. Dry and wet rubbing are different conditions; dark shades, pigment prints and brushed surfaces may be particularly sensitive.", "The moisture content of the rubbing cloth, load, stroke, number of cycles and staining assessment must follow the method. Do not wet the specimen by guesswork or use a rubbing cloth that does not meet the required specification.", "When results are low, examine unfixed surface colour, binder, washing, curing, pigment add-on, softener and pile construction. Identify whether the issue originates in the base fabric, print or finish.", "Technical basis and test scope. The rubbing test evaluates colour transfer from the specimen surface to a rubbing cloth under a specified load and motion. Dry and wet rubbing are separate conditions; moisture changes colour-transfer mechanisms and surface interactions. Raised constructions, surface pigments and finishing layers can produce different results.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Prepare the rubbing cloth to the correct type, dimensions and moisture condition required by the method. Secure the specimen without wrinkles and apply the specified rubbing direction and load. After the required number of cycles, assess staining on the rubbing cloth; also inspect changes to the specimen surface where required. Record dry and wet results separately, along with test face, direction and abnormalities such as fibre loss or surface polishing.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. If wet rubbing is markedly poorer, investigate unfixed dye, incompletely cured pigment layers or mobile finishing agents. Do not assign the cause solely to the base colour; compare dyeing or printing process records and lot data."], "deep": "Dry and wet rubbing tests assess colour transfer from the specimen surface to a rubbing cloth under a specified load and number of cycles. Rubbing-cloth moisture, pressure, speed and direction affect the result. Record dry and wet grades separately and describe the specimen surface condition. For pigment prints, coatings or dark shades, examine both pigment shedding and colour transfer. A dry-rubbing result cannot replace a wet-rubbing result.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Dry and Wet Rubbing Colourfastness\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Dry and Wet Rubbing Colourfastness</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Dry and Wet Rubbing Colourfastness</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Dry rubbing</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Wet rubbing</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Colour transfer</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Rating grade</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "21": {"title": "Perspiration Colourfastness Testing", "cat": "Colourfastness Testing", "body": ["Perspiration testing simulates contact with a solution of specified composition and pH. Depending on the method, both acidic and alkaline conditions may be assessed. The purpose is to evaluate colour change and staining after exposure.", "The solution composition, pH, exposure time, pressure, temperature and multifibre adjacent fabric must follow the specified standard. Do not use a self-mixed artificial perspiration solution unless its formulation is identical to the prescribed method.", "Assess staining on each adjacent fibre type separately, as well as colour change in the specimen. For skin-contact garments, also consider market-specific chemical and irritation requirements; a colourfastness test does not replace skin-safety assessment.", "Technical basis and test scope. Perspiration composition and pH vary with use conditions. The perspiration colourfastness test simulates material contact with a test solution under defined loading, time and temperature conditions. Results generally include both specimen colour change and staining transferred to adjacent material.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Prepare the solution in accordance with the method and verify its pH using checked equipment. Assemble the specimen and adjacent fabric in the required configuration, wet them evenly, remove excess solution as instructed and apply the specified pressure. After exposure, separate and dry the specimens as required before assessment. Record each solution or condition separately when the method calls for it.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Do not substitute an uncontrolled homemade solution. Incorrect pH or solution proportions can make results incomparable. For products worn next to the skin, interpret colourfastness together with chemical composition, finishing and the applicable market safety requirements."], "deep": "Perspiration testing uses a solution that simulates perspiration under specified composition and exposure conditions. It may assess specimen colour change and staining on adjacent material under acidic and alkaline conditions when required by the method. Prepare the solution to the correct formulation, pH and validity period; control pressure and exposure time. Distinguish colourfastness from chemical irritation or toxicity: these require separate assessment programmes.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Perspiration Colourfastness Testing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Perspiration Colourfastness Testing</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Perspiration Colourfastness Testing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Perspiration solution</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Contact</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Adjacent fabric</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assessment</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "22": {"title": "Light and Weathering Colourfastness Testing", "cat": "Colourfastness Testing", "body": ["Light testing uses a controlled radiation source and defined conditions to assess fading. Weathering tests may combine light, moisture and other environmental factors. Results depend on the method and reference standard used.", "Distinguish natural exposure from accelerated equipment testing. Do not convert exposure hours in a test chamber directly into months of outdoor use unless a validated correlation model is available.", "Select the test according to the application, such as upholstery, outdoor products, uniforms or fashion garments. Record the reference standard, exposure conditions and assessment scale.", "Technical basis and test scope. Light can degrade colourants and alter the polymer substrate. The rate of change depends on the radiation spectrum, intensity, temperature, humidity, oxygen and exposure time. Simulated light exposure in a test chamber is not identical to outdoor exposure because the spectrum and environmental cycles differ.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Define the light source, filters, reference standard, temperature and humidity conditions, exposure cycle and end point in accordance with the method. Mount specimens and references in the correct positions and avoid unintended shading. Record actual operating conditions, exposure duration and interruptions. Assess colour change using the required scale or instrument.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Reports must state the method and exposure conditions rather than simply describing lightfastness as good. Use the same light source and filters when comparing materials. For outdoor claims, consider field-use data or an appropriate weathering method; do not extrapolate years of service directly from a single accelerated test."], "deep": "Light and weathering tests use controlled exposure sources and conditions to assess colour change over time or at a defined exposure dose. Record the source type, filters, cycle, temperature, humidity and reference standard. Do not convert chamber hours into outdoor service time without a validated correlation model. For outdoor products, also consider mechanical durability and functional performance after exposure.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Light and Weathering Colourfastness Testing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Light and Weathering Colourfastness Testing</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Light and Weathering Colourfastness Testing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Light source</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Exposure cycle</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Reference standard</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Colour change</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "23": {"title": "Dimensional Stability After Laundering", "cat": "Dimensional Stability", "body": ["Dimensional stability measures changes in length and width after a specified washing and drying procedure. Woven fabrics, knits, elastane-containing materials and finished garments may respond differently.", "Mark measurement distances in accordance with the method, take initial measurements after conditioning, complete the specified wash and dry cycle, then recondition the specimen before final measurement. Do not measure a specimen while it is still hot or moisture-imbalanced.", "Report shrinkage or growth separately by direction and by specimen. Distinguish fabric dimensional change from spirality and from garment changes caused by seams or construction.", "Technical basis and test scope. Dimensional stability is affected by stress relaxation, fibre swelling and shrinkage, woven or knitted structure, mechanical and thermal finishing, and the laundering and drying programme. Lengthwise and widthwise changes may differ. Results must be linked to the number of cycles, washing and drying procedures, and conditioning state before and after treatment.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Mark the measurement distances using the prescribed layout and spacing, then take initial measurements after conditioning. Wash and dry using the specified programme without changing the load or detergent between tests. After treatment, recondition the specimen and measure the same marked points. Calculate percentage change using the method formula and retain the positive or negative sign to distinguish growth from shrinkage.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Illustrative calculation: a length reduction from 500 mm to 485 mm represents a change of -3%; this is an example of calculation, not an acceptance limit. If a result appears abnormal, check the marks, measuring tension, drying procedure and cycle count before drawing a conclusion."], "deep": "Measure dimensional stability by marking initial gauge distances in the lengthwise and widthwise directions, applying the specified care procedure and remeasuring after the required recovery period. Calculate percentage change using a consistent formula and sign convention. Report shrinkage and growth separately; do not collapse them into an absolute value when direction matters. For finished garments, also inspect seam twisting and distortion at the neck, sleeves and body.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Dimensional Stability After Laundering\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Dimensional Stability After Laundering</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Dimensional Stability After Laundering</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Marking</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Washing</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Conditioning</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Re-measurement</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "24": {"title": "Measurement of Spirality, Skewness and Bow", "cat": "Dimensional Stability", "body": ["Spirality commonly appears as twisting of wale lines or garment seams in knitted fabrics after processing. Skewness and bow describe the deviation of courses, wales or crosswise lines from their intended perpendicular or straight reference.", "Possible causes include fabric construction, yarn delivery, loop formation, tension, finishing, compacting and spreading or cutting practices. Reference points for fabric testing and garment testing must be defined separately.", "Assess the material after a laundering cycle representative of the intended care conditions. If the fabric passes but the garment twists, investigate pattern orientation, grainline, seam construction and fabric feeding on the production line.", "Technical basis and test scope. Spirality is commonly associated with body or side-seam twisting after laundering. Skewness describes the angular deviation of structural courses or wales from a perpendicular reference; bow describes curvature of a crosswise line across the fabric width. These characteristics can affect panel balance, seams and garment appearance, but their definitions must follow the applicable method.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Mark reference lines before treatment, lay the specimen without tension and photograph or measure it using a defined coordinate system. After laundering or other treatment, allow the specimen to relax, measure deviation at the specified positions and calculate it against the reference length. Record direction, fabric face, before-and-after condition and the formula used.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Do not use one generic twisting value as a substitute for all three types of deviation. If a side seam twists while the crosswise line remains straight, the cause may relate to knitted construction or yarn torque balance. If bow increases, investigate finishing and edge tension. Compare laboratory results with sewn samples to determine the practical garment effect."], "deep": "Spirality is the tendency of wale or course lines, or garment seams, to twist. Skewness and bow describe directional deviation of yarn systems or crosswise lines across the fabric width. Mark reference lines before treatment, spread the fabric without tension and measure according to the method. Distinguish fabric-structure defects from distortion introduced by cutting, sewing or laundering. In stripes and checks, these deviations can visibly affect matching across panels. Record direction, location and specimen condition.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Measurement of Spirality, Skewness and Bow\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Measurement of Spirality, Skewness and Bow</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Measurement of Spirality, Skewness and Bow</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Reference line</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Deviation</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Measurement position</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assessment</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "25": {"title": "Water Repellency, Spray Rating and Hydrostatic Head Testing", "cat": "Functional Performance", "body": ["The spray test evaluates surface wetting after water is applied as a spray. Hydrostatic head measures resistance to water penetration under water-column pressure. Water absorption measures the amount or rate of water uptake. These tests are not interchangeable.", "A fabric with a durable water-repellent (DWR) finish may achieve a good spray rating but still allow water to penetrate under pressure. A coated or membrane fabric may resist high water pressure but have lower water-vapour transmission or lose performance after abrasion.", "Select the test indicator according to the intended use and record specimen condition: as received, after laundering, after abrasion or after ageing. Do not make a waterproof claim based only on a spray test.", "Technical basis and test scope. Spray rating assesses surface wetting under defined spray conditions; hydrostatic head assesses resistance to water penetration under water-column pressure. Water penetration resistance at product level also depends on seams, zippers and construction interfaces. These three concepts must not be treated as equivalent.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Select the test according to the claim: surface water repellency, resistance to water penetration under pressure, or assessment of a complete product assembly. Prepare the correct specimen face, condition it and mount it without creating false leakage paths. Control pressure rise rate, test area, observation position and the criterion for identifying water penetration. For spray testing, maintain the specified distance, water quantity and spray angle.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Reports must identify the test type and conditions. A fabric with a good spray rating may still fail hydrostatic head; a DWR finish can reduce surface wetting but does not create a waterproof membrane. Do not use a fabric result to claim that a garment with untested seams is waterproof."], "deep": "Spray rating evaluates surface wetting by water droplets; hydrostatic head evaluates water penetration under pressure. Do not use these tests as substitutes for one another. Record the test face, pretreatment condition, pressure or pressure-rise rate and end point. For waterproof products, also test seams, zippers and joining points. A passing flat-fabric specimen does not establish that the finished garment is watertight.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Water Repellency, Spray Rating and Hydrostatic Head Testing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Water Repellency, Spray Rating and Hydrostatic Head Testing</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Water Repellency, Spray Rating and Hydrostatic Head Testing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Spray rating</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Hydrostatic head</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test face</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Finished garment</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "26": {"title": "Air Permeability and Water Vapour Transmission Testing", "cat": "Functional Performance", "body": ["Air permeability measures the volume of air passing through a material under a specified pressure differential. Water vapour transmission evaluates vapour transfer using a defined method; RET is one way of expressing evaporative resistance in certain test systems.", "Air permeability and water-vapour transmission are not equivalent. A membrane may block wind while still transmitting water vapour. Construction, temperature, humidity, pressure differential and test method determine the result.", "Check the method, units and test conditions before comparing suppliers. Evaluate the complete garment as well, because seams, linings, pockets and covered surface area can change actual performance.", "Technical basis and test scope. Air permeability measures airflow through a material under a defined pressure differential; water vapour transmission measures vapour transfer through a material under specified conditions. They describe different mechanisms: a fabric may resist airflow while allowing some water vapour to diffuse, or the reverse.", "Preparation, execution and required records. For air permeability, check fixture sealing, test area, pressure differential and specimen orientation; prevent air leakage around the edges. For WVTR, control the cup or instrument method, vapour-pressure differential, temperature, humidity and weighing or reading intervals. Record units, transfer direction, layer configuration and which specimen face is exposed to each environment.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Do not compare WVTR values from different methods unless their test conditions are equivalent. With multilayer materials, cut edges and bonded seams may create secondary transfer paths. Interpret results against the intended use and garment system; do not use a single indicator to claim overall comfort."], "deep": "Air permeability measures airflow through a material under a pressure differential; water vapour transmission measures vapour transfer under defined methods and conditions. A material may have low air permeability while retaining some water-vapour transmission. Record test area, pressure differential, temperature, humidity and test direction. For laminates, identify the exposed face and layer configuration. Do not combine the two indicators into one undefined concept of breathability.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Air Permeability and Water Vapour Transmission Testing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Air Permeability and Water Vapour Transmission Testing</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Air Permeability and Water Vapour Transmission Testing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Air permeability</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">WVTR</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test conditions</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Different mechanisms</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "27": {"title": "Absorbency, Wicking and Drying Tests", "cat": "Functional Performance", "body": ["Absorbency measures a material's ability to take up water; wicking evaluates liquid movement along or across the fabric; drying rate measures moisture loss under specified test conditions. A fabric may absorb quickly but retain water for a long time, or absorb little and dry quickly.", "Control the liquid quantity, specimen placement and direction, exposure time, airflow, temperature and humidity. Record the method and conditions because results from different instruments may not be directly comparable.", "For sportswear, consider garment comfort and test the multilayer construction where relevant. Do not infer the moisture-management performance of an entire shirt from a single wicking test on a fabric layer.", "Technical basis and test scope. Absorption, capillary transport and drying are three different stages. Absorbency describes the material's uptake of moisture or liquid; wicking describes liquid movement along the structure; drying describes the rate at which moisture leaves the material and enters the environment. Fibre composition, filament or fibre cross-section, construction, hydrophilic or hydrophobic finishes and thickness affect each stage.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Select the method that matches the product claim. For wicking, prepare strips in the specified direction, bring one end into contact with the liquid and record the height or mass of liquid movement over time. For drying, apply a consistent initial moisture quantity, control temperature, humidity and air velocity, and monitor mass over time. Keep the wetting procedure consistent across comparison specimens.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Fast wicking does not necessarily mean fast drying if the material retains a large quantity of water or has a dense construction. Review the time-based curve rather than only one final reading. Record the liquid type and environmental conditions because results may differ between pure water and simulated perspiration."], "deep": "Absorbency, wicking and drying describe different material behaviours. Absorbency concerns liquid uptake; wicking concerns liquid movement through the structure; drying tracks moisture loss over time. Select a test that matches the product claim. Record initial liquid quantity, specimen direction and area, temperature, humidity and airflow. Compare materials under the same conditions and finishing state.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Absorbency, Wicking and Drying Tests\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Absorbency, Wicking and Drying Tests</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Absorbency, Wicking and Drying Tests</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Absorbency</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Wicking</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Drying</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test conditions</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "28": {"title": "Stretch and Recovery Measurement", "cat": "Functional Performance", "body": ["Stretch measures the extent of deformation under force; recovery evaluates the ability to return towards the original dimensions after the load is removed; growth describes the residual deformation under the specified test conditions. These are separate indicators.", "Results depend on test direction, load, holding time, number of cycles, recovery time and measurement procedure. Elastane, knitted construction, heat exposure and finishing can have a substantial effect.", "Test both lengthwise and widthwise directions when the product requires multidirectional stretch. Test after laundering and ageing when required, and compare the results with actual fit and seam performance.", "Technical basis and test scope. Stretch describes deformation under force or during a prescribed extension test; recovery describes the ability to return towards the original dimensions after load removal and a defined rest period. A material can stretch considerably but recover poorly, causing bagging at knees, elbows or waist areas. Immediate extension must be distinguished from residual deformation.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Mark the original gauge length, apply the load or cycles specified by the method, and measure extension under load and after the prescribed recovery period. Record cycle count, speed, load, rest time and test direction. For two-way stretch materials, test both lengthwise and widthwise directions; for garments, consider panel construction and seams.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. When recovery is poor, investigate elastane, knitted structure, heat setting and laundering history. Do not conclude from maximum stretch alone; examine residual deformation after load removal. If fabric testing passes but the garment bags during wear, inspect seam construction, stitch density and load distribution across the product."], "deep": "Stretch and recovery testing requires control of extension, speed, holding time, cycle count and recovery time. Measure extension under load, residual deformation after unloading and recovery after the specified rest period. Lengthwise and widthwise behaviour may differ. Hand stretching is not a substitute for measurement because force and duration are uncontrolled. For elastane blends, record heat exposure and care history because they may affect results.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Stretch and Recovery Measurement\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Stretch and Recovery Measurement</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Stretch and Recovery Measurement</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Applied load</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Load holding</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Load removal</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Recovery</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "29": {"title": "Seam Strength Testing on Finished Garments", "cat": "Product Testing", "body": ["Garment testing should target representative load-bearing areas such as the crotch, underarm, sleeve opening, side seam or attachment points for trims. The test configuration must define gripping method, force direction and end point.", "Testing the base fabric does not replace seam testing. Garments contain multiple layers, seam intersections, bartacks and trims that create a stress distribution different from that of a flat specimen.", "When a failure occurs, photograph it and classify the failure mode: thread breakage, stitch slippage, fabric rupture, seam slippage or trim detachment. Use the findings to adjust construction and production methods.", "Technical basis and test scope. Seams in finished garments carry loads in their actual geometry, which may differ from a flat laboratory seam specimen. Pockets, underarms, crotches, shoulders and seam intersections create different stress states. The test specimen must therefore represent the product construction and manufacturing process.", "Preparation, execution and required records. Select test locations through use-risk analysis; record size, construction, materials, seam type, thread and machine settings. Define the gripping arrangement so that force is transmitted through the seam without specimen slippage or artificial stress concentration. Record force, opening, failure mode and initiation location. If the test destroys a garment, define replacement specimens and lot sampling procedures.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. When failure occurs at a seam intersection, examine stitch density, backtacking, stacked-layer thickness and sewing sequence. Finished-garment results should be reviewed together with inline and end-line inspection data; destructive testing does not replace in-process controls."], "deep": "Seam testing on finished garments must target locations representative of actual loads and construction. Record size, seam type, stitch density, thread, fabric layers and post-laundering condition. Observe breakage, thread slippage, fabric rupture, seam opening or trim deformation. Coupon testing does not fully replace finished-garment testing because geometry and stress distribution differ. Retain photographs of the test location and product identification.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Seam Strength Testing on Finished Garments\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Seam Strength Testing on Finished Garments</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Seam Strength Testing on Finished Garments</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Garment location</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Service load</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Seam test</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Failure mode</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "30": {"title": "Broken Needle and Metal Contamination Control", "cat": "Product Testing", "body": ["A broken-needle control programme must define machine stoppage, product isolation, recovery of all fragments, needle identification and inspection of the affected area. This is a product-safety control, not merely a paperwork procedure.", "Metal detection or needle detection must be validated for the product, inspection position, metal construction and buyer requirements. Sensitivity settings must be challenged using standard test pieces in accordance with the procedure.", "Retain inspection records at the start of each shift, after changes or repairs, and whenever a foreign object is detected. Do not release goods until the incident has been fully resolved under the established procedure.", "Technical basis and test scope. Broken-needle control is a traceable system for managing metal fragments that may originate from sewing machines; it is more than locating the missing part of a needle. Risk increases when fragments are not fully recovered, products continue moving through production, or records fail to identify the potentially affected quantity.", "Preparation, execution and required records. When a needle breaks, stop the machine and isolate products made since the last safe check, as defined by the SOP. Collect all fragments, reconcile their shape and number, and inspect the machine area, table, floor and products. Record machine ID, operator, time, lot, quarantined products and the person verifying the search. Release products only after the search and approval process is complete.", "Interpreting results and limits of conclusion. Verify metal detectors using standard test pieces suitable for the system and inspection position. Check sensitivity, conveyor speed, spacing and product presentation. Do not claim that a product is absolutely free of metal; the report must describe the detection capability and the procedure applied."], "deep": "Broken-needle and metal-contamination control requires a procedure covering detection, line stoppage, product containment, fragment recovery and equipment verification. Verify the metal detector's detection capability using suitable test pieces before and after shifts or according to the control plan. Record machine ID, sensitivity, product type, challenge-test result and action taken after failure. Distinguish broken-needle control from control of every possible foreign object.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Broken Needle and Metal Contamination Control\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Broken Needle and Metal Contamination Control</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Broken Needle and Metal Contamination Control</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Needle detection</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test piece</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Product containment</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Records</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "31": {"title": "Appearance, Colour and Metamerism Assessment", "cat": "Colour Measurement and Appearance", "body": ["Colour assessment requires controlled lighting, background, viewing angle, specimen orientation and comparison standard. Metamerism occurs when two samples look the same under one light source but differ under another.", "Distinguish visual assessment from instrumental colour measurement. The instrument record must state the measurement geometry, illuminant/observer, aperture, SCI/SCE where applicable, number of readings and how they were averaged.", "When approving a shade, view it under several light sources relevant to end use and retail, and check the face side, pile direction and lustre. Reference standards must be protected from light, heat and contamination.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** Appearance depends on colour, lustre, texture, pile direction, transparency, opacity and lighting conditions. Metamerism occurs when two samples appear the same under one light source but differ under another because their spectral reflectance curves differ. Colour assessment therefore cannot rely on a single lamp or a display screen.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Prepare the standard and the test specimen clean, flat and in the same viewing direction. Use a light cabinet with specified illuminants, a neutral background and consistent viewing conditions. Assess under the light sources relevant to the target market or end-use environment; record the illuminant, the assessor, the specimen face and the degree of difference. Combine visual assessment with instrument data when the spec requires it.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** When a sample matches under D65 but differs under store lighting, investigate the spectral reflectance curves and the dye recipe. Do not try to correct the colour against a single lighting condition if the buyer requires several light sources. Keep the standard under control and record the replacement history of the standard."], "deep": "Appearance and metamerism assessment requires control of the light source, background, viewing angle, specimen size and eye adaptation time. Metamerism occurs when two samples match under one source but differ under another. Use a light box with the specified illuminants and record the viewing source. Compare the bulk sample with the standard in the same finished state. Do not use a phone photograph as the only colour reference, because white balance and display screens vary.\n\n**Technical analysis:** Appearance depends on colour, lustre, texture, pile direction, transparency, opacity and lighting conditions. Metamerism occurs when two samples appear the same under one light source but differ under another because their spectral reflectance curves differ. Colour assessment therefore cannot rely on a single lamp or a display screen.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Prepare the standard and the test specimen clean, flat and in the same viewing direction. Use a light cabinet with specified illuminants, a neutral background and consistent viewing conditions. Assess under the light sources relevant to the target market or end-use environment; record the illuminant, the assessor, the specimen face and the degree of difference. Combine visual assessment with instrument data when the spec requires it.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** When a sample matches under D65 but differs under store lighting, investigate the spectral reflectance curves and the dye recipe. Do not try to correct the colour against a single lighting condition if the buyer requires several light sources. Keep the standard under control and record the replacement history of the standard.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Appearance, Colour and Metamerism Assessment\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Appearance, Colour and Metamerism Assessment</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Appearance, Colour and Metamerism Assessment</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Light source</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Light box</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Comparison</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Metamerism</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "32": {"title": "Spectrophotometric Colour Measurement and ΔE", "cat": "Colour Measurement and Appearance", "body": ["A spectrophotometer records spectral reflectance and calculates colour coordinates in the selected colour system. ΔE expresses colour difference according to a specific formula; ΔE76, ΔE94 and ΔE00 are not equivalent.", "The illuminant, observer, colour space, ΔE formula, measurement conditions, aperture, number of readings and specimen rotation must be recorded. Instrument results do not fully replace visual assessment for materials with texture or special effects.", "Do not use one common ΔE threshold for every colour, material and buyer. Confirm the tolerance through agreement and an approved sample; retain spectral data in case a shade investigation is needed.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** A spectrophotometer measures spectral reflectance and calculates colour coordinates according to the observer/illuminant system that has been set up. ΔE expresses colour distance according to the selected formula; different formulae have different sensitivity in different regions of colour space and under different conditions. A ΔE value is therefore meaningful only when accompanied by the measurement configuration and the standard.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Calibrate the instrument according to the instructions; define the measurement geometry, aperture, specular mode, illuminant, observer and number of readings. Place the specimen flat, avoiding creases, pile direction and uncontrolled see-through backing. Measure multiple positions according to the plan, rotate the specimen if the method requires it, and store the spectral data, coordinates and ΔE. State clearly the standard used and the formula version.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** Do not compare ΔE values from two different settings as if they were on the same scale. Patterned fabrics or non-uniform surfaces need multiple measurement points and supplementary visual assessment. When ΔE passes but the eye still sees a difference, consider metamerism, lustre, texture and the difference between the physical standard and the digital data."], "deep": "A spectrophotometer measures colour reflectance and calculates colour coordinates; ΔE expresses the difference according to the colour-space formula selected. The report must state the colour system, illuminant, observer, measurement geometry, aperture, number of readings and ΔE formula. Results depend on surface, fibre direction, lustre and backing. For textured fabrics, measure several positions and rotate the specimen as specified. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification/buyer; do not assume a common threshold.\n\n**Technical analysis:** A spectrophotometer measures spectral reflectance and calculates colour coordinates according to the observer/illuminant system that has been set up. ΔE expresses colour distance according to the selected formula; different formulae have different sensitivity in different regions of colour space and under different conditions. A ΔE value is therefore meaningful only when accompanied by the measurement configuration and the standard.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Calibrate the instrument according to the instructions; define the measurement geometry, aperture, specular mode, illuminant, observer and number of readings. Place the specimen flat, avoiding creases, pile direction and uncontrolled see-through backing. Measure multiple positions according to the plan, rotate the specimen if the method requires it, and store the spectral data, coordinates and ΔE. State clearly the standard used and the formula version.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** Do not compare ΔE values from two different settings as if they were on the same scale. Patterned fabrics or non-uniform surfaces need multiple measurement points and supplementary visual assessment. When ΔE passes but the eye still sees a difference, consider metamerism, lustre, texture and the difference between the physical standard and the digital data.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Spectrophotometric Colour Measurement and ΔE\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Spectrophotometric Colour Measurement and ΔE</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Spectrophotometric Colour Measurement and ΔE</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Colour instrument</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Colour space</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">ΔE</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Criteria</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "33": {"title": "Whiteness, Yellowness and Gloss Assessment", "cat": "Colour Measurement and Appearance", "body": ["Whiteness and yellowness indices are calculated according to a specific formula and measurement conditions. Optical brightening agents can cause results to vary with the UV source of the instrument.", "Gloss depends on the measurement angle, surface orientation, structure and substrate lustre. For fabrics with hairiness, pile or texture, the orientation and specimen placement must be specified.", "When investigating yellowing, check heat, light, chemicals, pH, metals, oils, packaging and storage conditions. Compare with a retained sample that has been stored correctly.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** Whiteness and yellowness are optical properties that depend on spectral reflectance, optical brightening agents, the fibre substrate and the light source. Gloss reflects the proportion of directionally reflected light and is influenced by structure, calendering, coating and surface orientation. The three quantities cannot be combined into a single remark such as \"bright white\".", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Select the instrument and method according to the property to be measured; check the light source configuration, geometry, backing and calibration standard. For whiteness/yellowness, record the index system, observer and measurement conditions; for gloss, define the measurement angle and surface orientation. Prepare specimens in the same state, and do not flatten or brush the surface differently between the standard and the test specimen.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** Optical brightening agents can cause results to vary with the UV content of the light source. When instrument results and visual assessment disagree, check the UV configuration and the lighting conditions. Do not compare indices between different measurement systems unless the correlation has been confirmed."], "deep": "Whiteness, yellowness and gloss are different parameters and need separate instrument configurations and methods. Whiteness/yellowness indices depend on the illuminant and observer; gloss depends on the measurement angle and the surface. Record the specimen state, backing, orientation, number of readings and instrument. For white fabric, check both the selvedge area and the middle of the roll to detect localised yellowing. Do not substitute a visual impression under one light source for a measurement when the specification requires one.\n\n**Technical analysis:** Whiteness and yellowness are optical properties that depend on spectral reflectance, optical brightening agents, the fibre substrate and the light source. Gloss reflects the proportion of directionally reflected light and is influenced by structure, calendering, coating and surface orientation. The three quantities cannot be combined into a single remark such as \"bright white\".\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Select the instrument and method according to the property to be measured; check the light source configuration, geometry, backing and calibration standard. For whiteness/yellowness, record the index system, observer and measurement conditions; for gloss, define the measurement angle and surface orientation. Prepare specimens in the same state, and do not flatten or brush the surface differently between the standard and the test specimen.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** Optical brightening agents can cause results to vary with the UV content of the light source. When instrument results and visual assessment disagree, check the UV configuration and the lighting conditions. Do not compare indices between different measurement systems unless the correlation has been confirmed.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Whiteness, Yellowness and Gloss Assessment\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Whiteness, Yellowness and Gloss Assessment</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Whiteness, Yellowness and Gloss Assessment</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Whiteness</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Yellowness</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Gloss</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Measurement</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "34": {"title": "Restricted Substances Testing and RSL", "cat": "Chemicals and Compliance", "body": ["An RSL is a list of substances restricted in the finished product; an MRSL focuses on input chemicals used in the production process. The two lists have different purposes and control points.", "The analytes depend on the market, product type, skin contact, age group, material and version of the list. The laboratory must use a suitable method with a quantification limit low enough relative to the applicable limit.", "Read the report by analyte, unit, LOD/LOQ, quantified result, sample scope and RSL version. Do not conclude 'substance absent' when the result only shows a value below the detection limit.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** An RSL (Restricted Substances List) defines the substances restricted in finished goods or materials within the scope of a buyer, brand or market. An RSL differs from an MRSL in what it controls: an RSL focuses on the product, whereas an MRSL generally addresses input chemicals and the production process. Input-chemical certification cannot replace finished-product test results when the requirement calls for product testing.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Define the applicable list, market, product category, material, component and document version. Build a matrix of substance, at-risk material, analytical method, limit according to the controlling document and sampling plan. Select a laboratory whose competence suits the analyte and the sample matrix. Record the chain of custody, composite samples where permitted, and all detection/quantification limits.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** Do not interpret \"not detected\" as \"not present\"; the result depends on the limits of the method. When the supplier, chemicals, colour or process step changes, reassess the risk. Do not copy limits from one market to another until the legal scope and effective date have been confirmed."], "deep": "An RSL controls restricted substances remaining in the product; testing must be based on the applicable list, material, colour, finish, market and date. Define the analyte, sample matrix, method, reporting limit and sampling approach. Check the laboratory's scope of accreditation and whether the report covers the substances actually required. A \"not detected\" result depends on the detection/quantification limit and does not mean zero.\n\n**Technical analysis:** An RSL (Restricted Substances List) defines the substances restricted in finished goods or materials within the scope of a buyer, brand or market. An RSL differs from an MRSL in what it controls: an RSL focuses on the product, whereas an MRSL generally addresses input chemicals and the production process. Input-chemical certification cannot replace finished-product test results when the requirement calls for product testing.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Define the applicable list, market, product category, material, component and document version. Build a matrix of substance, at-risk material, analytical method, limit according to the controlling document and sampling plan. Select a laboratory whose competence suits the analyte and the sample matrix. Record the chain of custody, composite samples where permitted, and all detection/quantification limits.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** Do not interpret \"not detected\" as \"not present\"; the result depends on the limits of the method. When the supplier, chemicals, colour or process step changes, reassess the risk. Do not copy limits from one market to another until the legal scope and effective date have been confirmed.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Restricted Substances Testing and RSL\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Restricted Substances Testing and RSL</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Restricted Substances Testing and RSL</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">RSL list</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Sampling</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Analysis</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Conclusion</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "35": {"title": "Formaldehyde, pH and Metals Assessment", "cat": "Chemicals and Compliance", "body": ["Formaldehyde, extract pH and metals can be key parameter groups depending on the product and the market. The extraction method, sample-to-solution ratio, time and measurement procedure must be defined.", "Legal limits can differ by country, age group and product type. Do not rely on an outdated limit table as the sole basis; check the regulations and buyer requirements currently in force.", "When a result is abnormal, check finishing chemicals, dyes, trims, water, packaging and cross-contamination. Take confirmation samples according to the procedure and retain the chain of custody.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** Formaldehyde, pH and metals are different groups of chemical parameters, each with its own mechanism of origin, analytical method and reporting unit. Formaldehyde may be associated with certain finishing systems; pH reflects the acidity/alkalinity of an extract under specified conditions; metals may be present as total content or as an extractable fraction depending on the method.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Do not use one common sample preparation procedure for all three groups. For each analyte, define the sample matrix, extraction/digestion method, analytical instrument, blank, and suitable spiked sample or control material. Record the units, mass basis, detection/quantification limits and, where the method requires it, recovery. Compare results with the requirements that actually apply to the product and the market.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** A pH result does not replace the assessment of restricted chemicals; total metal content does not mean the metal is capable of leaching. When a result is abnormal, review the blank, the equipment, cross-contamination and the chemical records before drawing a conclusion on the lot."], "deep": "Formaldehyde, pH and metals are analyses that differ in sample preparation, instrumentation and interpretation. Define the sample type, extraction, time, solvent ratio and quantification method. For metals, distinguish total content from the extractable/released fraction as required. The report must give units, LOQ/LOD, measurement uncertainty where applicable and the reference criteria. Do not conclude compliance from a single parameter if the requirement covers several substances.\n\n**Technical analysis:** Formaldehyde, pH and metals are different groups of chemical parameters, each with its own mechanism of origin, analytical method and reporting unit. Formaldehyde may be associated with certain finishing systems; pH reflects the acidity/alkalinity of an extract under specified conditions; metals may be present as total content or as an extractable fraction depending on the method.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Do not use one common sample preparation procedure for all three groups. For each analyte, define the sample matrix, extraction/digestion method, analytical instrument, blank, and suitable spiked sample or control material. Record the units, mass basis, detection/quantification limits and, where the method requires it, recovery. Compare results with the requirements that actually apply to the product and the market.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** A pH result does not replace the assessment of restricted chemicals; total metal content does not mean the metal is capable of leaching. When a result is abnormal, review the blank, the equipment, cross-contamination and the chemical records before drawing a conclusion on the lot.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Formaldehyde, pH and Metals Assessment\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Formaldehyde, pH and Metals Assessment</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Formaldehyde, pH and Metals Assessment</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Sample preparation</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Extraction</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Quantification</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Report</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "36": {"title": "Extract pH and Electrical Conductivity Testing", "cat": "Chemicals and Compliance", "body": ["Extract pH reflects the acidity or alkalinity of the extract obtained under the method; it is not a direct measurement of pH at individual points on the fibre. The specimen-to-solution ratio and water quality affect the result.", "Electrical conductivity can help to detect salts or dissolved ionic substances, but it cannot identify specific chemicals. Do not use conductivity as a substitute for analysis of restricted substances.", "Calibrate the pH meter with suitable buffers, and check the electrode, temperature and stabilisation time. Record the water type, extraction ratio, shaking time and temperature.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** Extract pH reflects the hydrogen-ion activity of the extract; electrical conductivity reflects the ability of dissolved ions to carry current. The two parameters are related but not equivalent: the same pH can have different conductivity because of differing ion concentration and ion type. Results depend on the specimen-to-solution ratio, the water used, extraction time and temperature.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Prepare specimens to the specified size and mass; use clean water and clean apparatus, and avoid ionic contamination from containers or the operator's hands. Record the extraction ratio, time, temperature and method of agitation. Calibrate the pH meter with suitable buffer solutions; check the cell constant or verify the conductivity meter according to the SOP. Measure the blank and the test specimens by the same procedure.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** Do not compare results obtained with different extraction ratios or water types. If pH varies widely between tests, check solution stability, reading time and electrode calibration. High conductivity may suggest a large amount of salts or dissolved ions, but it cannot determine the specific chemical composition."], "deep": "Extract pH depends on the specimen-to-solution ratio, water type, extraction time, temperature and method of agitation. Electrical conductivity reflects total dissolved ions but does not identify individual substances. Standardise the water, calibrate the electrode and instrument, rinse the apparatus and run a blank where necessary. Record the measurement temperature, because pH and conductivity can be temperature-dependent. When results are abnormal, check the water, residual chemicals and cross-contamination of apparatus.\n\n**Technical analysis:** Extract pH reflects the hydrogen-ion activity of the extract; electrical conductivity reflects the ability of dissolved ions to carry current. The two parameters are related but not equivalent: the same pH can have different conductivity because of differing ion concentration and ion type. Results depend on the specimen-to-solution ratio, the water used, extraction time and temperature.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Prepare specimens to the specified size and mass; use clean water and clean apparatus, and avoid ionic contamination from containers or the operator's hands. Record the extraction ratio, time, temperature and method of agitation. Calibrate the pH meter with suitable buffer solutions; check the cell constant or verify the conductivity meter according to the SOP. Measure the blank and the test specimens by the same procedure.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** Do not compare results obtained with different extraction ratios or water types. If pH varies widely between tests, check solution stability, reading time and electrode calibration. High conductivity may suggest a large amount of salts or dissolved ions, but it cannot determine the specific chemical composition.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, tester, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, in itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Extract pH and Electrical Conductivity Testing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Extract pH and Electrical Conductivity Testing</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Extract pH and Electrical Conductivity Testing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Extract</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">pH</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Conductivity</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">QC</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "37": {"title": "Flame Resistance and Protective Heat Testing", "cat": "Safety Performance", "body": ["Flammability testing of textiles depends on end use: nightwear, protective clothing, curtains, furnishing materials and industrial PPE each have different requirements. The correct standard and specimen configuration must be selected.", "Depending on the test, evaluate burning time, flame spread, afterflame, afterglow, char length, molten dripping or heat transfer. A single test does not prove every level of protection.", "Do not test in an area without dedicated safety equipment and procedures. The report should record specimen orientation, layer construction, pre-treatment washing and any conditions that may influence the result.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** Flame-resistance and protective-heat tests must be tied to a specific use hazard: flame contact, convective heat, radiant heat, contact with hot surfaces or electric arc. A material that self-extinguishes in a small-scale test is not automatically suitable for every protective application. Multilayer construction, seams and trims can change the behaviour of the complete garment.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Identify the standard and version, protection level, specimen configuration, test direction, pre-treatment conditions and evaluation criteria. Test the material and/or the assembly, as required by the scope; record burning behaviour, flame spread, molten dripping, shrinkage, heat transfer and damage. Equipment, test chambers and safety procedures must be operated by competent personnel.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** Do not declare a protection level from a single parameter. For PPE, check the legal requirements, conformity assessment and certification scope of the finished product. Changing the fibre, coating, sewing thread or seam construction can invalidate earlier evidence."], "deep": "Flame-resistance and protective-heat tests must be selected according to the use hazard and product configuration. Tests may assess flame spread, burning time, heat transfer, molten drips or heat resistance; results are not interchangeable. Record specimen orientation, structural layers, trims, seams and condition after washing. Certification of the base fabric does not automatically prove that the complete garment meets the protective requirements.\n\n**Technical analysis:** Flame-resistance and protective-heat tests must be tied to a specific use hazard: flame contact, convective heat, radiant heat, contact with hot surfaces or electric arc. A material that self-extinguishes in a small-scale test is not automatically suitable for every protective application. Multilayer construction, seams and trims can change the behaviour of the complete garment.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Identify the standard and version, protection level, specimen configuration, test direction, pre-treatment conditions and evaluation criteria. Test the material and/or the assembly, as required by the scope; record burning behaviour, flame spread, molten dripping, shrinkage, heat transfer and damage. Equipment, test chambers and safety procedures must be operated by competent personnel.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** Do not declare a protection level from a single parameter. For PPE, check the legal requirements, conformity assessment and certification scope of the finished product. Changing the fibre, coating, sewing thread or seam construction can invalidate earlier evidence.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, tester, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, in itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Flame Resistance and Protective Heat Testing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Flame Resistance and Protective Heat Testing</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Flame Resistance and Protective Heat Testing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Use hazard</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Specimen set-up</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Conclusion</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "38": {"title": "Product Performance Testing After Washing and Ageing", "cat": "Product Testing", "body": ["A product may pass when new but deteriorate after washing, abrasion, UV, heat or repeated use. The verification plan must simulate a reasonable product life cycle and the care conditions stated on the label.", "Select the number of cycles, detergent, load, temperature, drying method and specimen condition according to the standard or buyer requirement. Do not convert a number of test cycles into a number of wears without a justified basis.", "Measure critical parameters before and after treatment: colour, dimensions, strength, function, delamination, pilling and appearance. Retain an untreated control specimen for root-cause analysis.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** Performance after washing or ageing assesses the ability to retain function over a simulated period of use. Exposures such as washing, drying, light, heat, perspiration and abrasion can interact; results depend on the sequence of exposures and the number of cycles. Testing each exposure separately does not always predict the combined effect.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Identify the claim to be maintained and the corresponding before-and-after measurement: dimensions, colour, water resistance, stretch, seam strength or appearance. Prepare control specimens, lock the washing or ageing programme, and record the number of cycles and the conditions between stages. After treatment, recondition the specimens and apply the same measurement method as at the start so that the data are comparable.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** Report the initial value, the value after treatment and the change, not just the final state. If performance declines, analyse whether the cause is the material, coating, bonding, trims or care procedure. A durability claim must rest on an appropriate test basis, and laboratory cycles must not be converted arbitrarily into real service time."], "deep": "Testing after washing or ageing must simulate the relevant conditions of use: number of cycles, heat, chemicals, friction, UV, humidity or flexing. Measure properties before and after on equivalent specimens and record the change. For functional claims, determine which performance must be retained after care. Do not infer real-life durability from a single accelerated cycle without an established correlation. Retain control specimens and the treatment history.\n\n**Technical analysis:** Performance after washing or ageing assesses the ability to retain function over a simulated period of use. Exposures such as washing, drying, light, heat, perspiration and abrasion can interact; results depend on the sequence of exposures and the number of cycles. Testing each exposure separately does not always predict the combined effect.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Identify the claim to be maintained and the corresponding before-and-after measurement: dimensions, colour, water resistance, stretch, seam strength or appearance. Prepare control specimens, lock the washing or ageing programme, and record the number of cycles and the conditions between stages. After treatment, recondition the specimens and apply the same measurement method as at the start so that the data are comparable.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** Report the initial value, the value after treatment and the change, not just the final state. If performance declines, analyse whether the cause is the material, coating, bonding, trims or care procedure. A durability claim must rest on an appropriate test basis, and laboratory cycles must not be converted arbitrarily into real service time.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, tester, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, in itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Product Performance Testing After Washing and Ageing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Product Performance Testing After Washing and Ageing</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Product Performance Testing After Washing and Ageing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Ageing</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Washing</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Before/after</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Performance durability</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "39": {"title": "Trim and Material Compatibility Testing", "cat": "Product Testing", "body": ["Zips, buttons, hook-and-loop tape, printed labels, adhesives, elastic, coatings and threads can react differently when exposed to heat, water, chemicals or mechanical force.", "Test compatibility along the real process chain: pressing, sewing, washing, drying, ironing and storage. A trim that passes when tested alone can still cause migration, colour staining, adhesive failure or corrosion in combination.", "Prepare a list of approved materials and suppliers; when a code or source of supply changes, determine which re-verification tests are required.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** Compatibility must be considered at assembly level: main fabric, interlining, thread, zip, button, label, elastic, coating and finishing chemicals may interact during pressing, sewing, washing or use. A trim that passes on its own can still fail when combined with the base fabric, for example through colour transfer, corrosion, adhesive failure, differential shrinkage or surface damage.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Build a matrix of material pairs by position of use and exposure condition. Prepare composite specimens that reproduce the product construction; record lot number, supplier, contact face, material direction and assembly procedure. Test in the initial state and after relevant exposures such as pressing heat, washing, perspiration, abrasion or ageing. Select tests according to actual risk; do not apply the whole test suite mechanically.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** When staining or delamination appears, compare the individual specimens with the combined specimen to identify the interaction. Keep before-and-after photographs, the location of the defect, the test conditions and the material lots. The conclusion applies only to the assembly and conditions tested; changing the supplier, adhesive formulation, dye or pressing temperature may change the result."], "deep": "Trims must be tested for compatibility with the fabric and the process: shrinkage, colour bleeding, corrosion, adhesive failure, heat distortion, tensile strength and care. Test the real assembly, including thread, zip, button, interlining, label, elastic, ink and main material. Evaluate before and after washing or pressing under conditions of use. A trim that passes individually can still cause failure when combined with the base fabric or finishing chemicals.\n\n**Technical analysis:** Compatibility must be considered at assembly level: main fabric, interlining, thread, zip, button, label, elastic, coating and finishing chemicals may interact during pressing, sewing, washing or use. A trim that passes on its own can still fail when combined with the base fabric, for example through colour transfer, corrosion, adhesive failure, differential shrinkage or surface damage.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Build a matrix of material pairs by position of use and exposure condition. Prepare composite specimens that reproduce the product construction; record lot number, supplier, contact face, material direction and assembly procedure. Test in the initial state and after relevant exposures such as pressing heat, washing, perspiration, abrasion or ageing. Select tests according to actual risk; do not apply the whole test suite mechanically.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** When staining or delamination appears, compare the individual specimens with the combined specimen to identify the interaction. Keep before-and-after photographs, the location of the defect, the test conditions and the material lots. The conclusion applies only to the assembly and conditions tested; changing the supplier, adhesive formulation, dye or pressing temperature may change the result.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, tester, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, in itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Trim and Material Compatibility Testing\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Trim and Material Compatibility Testing</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Trim and Material Compatibility Testing</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Main material</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Trims</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assembly test</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Verification</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "40": {"title": "Test Reports and How to Read Results", "cat": "Reporting and Analysis", "body": ["A report must uniquely identify the sample, the customer or lot where permitted, date received, date tested, method and version, test conditions, results, units, deviations and approver.", "Distinguish measured results, observational remarks and pass/fail conclusions. A conclusion is meaningful only when a specification and the corresponding decision rule exist.", "Do not alter original results. If a revised report is issued, record the version, the reason, the changed sections and a link to the previous report.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** A test report is a traceable, reproducible record, not a single “pass/fail” line. The reader needs to know which sample was tested, which method was used, which conditions were applied, how the results were calculated and what limits apply to the conclusion. Numerical results must be accompanied by units, number of specimens, number of measurements and rounding rules consistent with the method.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Minimum checks: sample ID and condition on receipt; method and version; test date; conditioning; equipment; operator; raw data; calculations; deviations from the method; photographs or observations of abnormalities; reviewer and releasing person. Match the specification requirement to the correct property and the correct method; do not compare a measured result with a criterion belonging to a different test.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** For example, dry and wet colourfastness to rubbing are two separate results; do not merge them into one average rating if the evaluation rule does not allow it. If a report carries a “deviation” note, read the description before using the report for approval. When a result is close to the limit or carries significant uncertainty, the report must pass that information to the decision-maker rather than hide it behind a pass/fail label."], "deep": "A test report must identify the sample, method and version, dates received and tested, conditions, equipment, results, units, criteria and approver. Distinguish measured results from remarks, pass/fail conclusions and scope of application. Record every deviation from the method, limits of the report and information not supplied. Do not separate the conclusion page from the original report when sending it to the customer; keep the link to the raw data.\n\n**Technical analysis:** A test report is a traceable, reproducible record, not a single “pass/fail” line. The reader needs to know which sample was tested, which method was used, which conditions were applied, how the results were calculated and what limits apply to the conclusion. Numerical results must be accompanied by units, number of specimens, number of measurements and rounding rules consistent with the method.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Minimum checks: sample ID and condition on receipt; method and version; test date; conditioning; equipment; operator; raw data; calculations; deviations from the method; photographs or observations of abnormalities; reviewer and releasing person. Match the specification requirement to the correct property and the correct method; do not compare a measured result with a criterion belonging to a different test.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** For example, dry and wet colourfastness to rubbing are two separate results; do not merge them into one average rating if the evaluation rule does not allow it. If a report carries a “deviation” note, read the description before using the report for approval. When a result is close to the limit or carries significant uncertainty, the report must pass that information to the decision-maker rather than hide it behind a pass/fail label.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, tester, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, in itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Test Reports and How to Read Results\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Test Reports and How to Read Results</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Test Reports and How to Read Results</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Sample ID</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Method</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Result</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Approval</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "41": {"title": "Handling Abnormal Results and Retesting", "cat": "Reporting and Analysis", "body": ["When a result is abnormal, first check its validity: specimen identification, pretreatment, equipment, reagents, environmental conditions, handling and calculations. Only then decide whether to take a new sample or retest.", "Distinguish a planned retest from a repeat caused by an operator error. Do not repeat a test several times and select only the passing result; the treatment of outliers must be defined in advance and supported by evidence.", "If the lot is suspected of being non-uniform, extend sampling under controlled conditions. Keep both the initial failing result and the confirmatory result to avoid reporting bias.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** An abnormal result may be a statistical outlier, an operator error, specimen damage, an equipment fault or genuine variation in the material. Retesting is not a way of continuing to measure until a passing result appears. Every decision to retest must rest on a verifiable cause and on rules defined in advance.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Preserve the original data; open an abnormal-result record with sample ID, method, equipment, operator, conditions and observed signs. Check the raw data, control samples, equipment status and handling. If there are grounds to invalidate the run, record the reason and the approver; if not, report the original result and plan the sampling/retest according to the approved rules.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** Distinguish between a retest on the same specimen, testing a new specimen and resampling from the lot; each answers a different question. Do not replace the original result with a later passing result without stating the history. When results conflict, investigate the variation and assess the extent of the affected lot."], "deep": "When a result is abnormal, first check the sample ID, label, conditions, equipment, calculation and data entry. Establish whether the cause may be an operator error, equipment, a non-uniform sample or the production process. Retest only when there are grounds to do so, and record the reason, whether a new or retained sample is used, the number of tests and the reporting rule. Do not repeat until a passing result appears and then discard the failing one; keep the whole history so that the decision is transparent.\n\n**Technical analysis:** An abnormal result may be a statistical outlier, an operator error, specimen damage, an equipment fault or genuine variation in the material. Retesting is not a way of continuing to measure until a passing result appears. Every decision to retest must rest on a verifiable cause and on rules defined in advance.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Preserve the original data; open an abnormal-result record with sample ID, method, equipment, operator, conditions and observed signs. Check the raw data, control samples, equipment status and handling. If there are grounds to invalidate the run, record the reason and the approver; if not, report the original result and plan the sampling/retest according to the approved rules.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** Distinguish between a retest on the same specimen, testing a new specimen and resampling from the lot; each answers a different question. Do not replace the original result with a later passing result without stating the history. When results conflict, investigate the variation and assess the extent of the affected lot.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Handling Abnormal Results and Retesting\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Handling Abnormal Results and Retesting</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Handling Abnormal Results and Retesting</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Specimen check</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Equipment check</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Retest</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">History</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "42": {"title": "Trend Analysis and Statistical Control", "cat": "Reporting and Analysis", "body": ["Laboratory data over time can reveal drift in a supplier, machine, chemical or process step. Monitor the mean, dispersion, failure rate and trends by style/lot.", "Do not use control charts when the data do not satisfy the assumptions or the sample size is too small. Distinguish the process control limits from the customer's specification limits.", "When an abnormal signal appears, check changes in material, equipment, shift, operator and environmental conditions. Use the data to open an investigation; do not automatically attribute a cause.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** Laboratory data can be used to detect drift in materials, suppliers, equipment or processes. However, a statistical chart is meaningful only when the data share the same definition, method, units and conditions. Mixing data from different method versions or material groups can create false signals.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Standardise product codes, suppliers, lots, tests, methods and timing. Distinguish continuous data from attribute data; choose a chart suited to the sampling structure and frequency. Set the centre line and control limits from stable process data using an approved statistical procedure; do not confuse control limits with specification limits.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** When a point falls outside control or a run of trending points appears, open a root-cause investigation before adjusting the process. Do not remove a data point merely because it worsens a KPI. Keep the reason for exclusion, the approver and the original data. A dashboard must allow drill-down to the test report and the material lot."], "deep": "Trend analysis requires data with the same method, units, scope and definitions. Monitor the mean, dispersion, failure rate, turnaround time and defect categories by supplier/style. Control charts are appropriate only when the data and the process meet the assumptions of the method. Distinguish control limits from specification limits; a process in control does not mean that the product meets customer requirements.\n\n**Technical analysis:** Laboratory data can be used to detect drift in materials, suppliers, equipment or processes. However, a statistical chart is meaningful only when the data share the same definition, method, units and conditions. Mixing data from different method versions or material groups can create false signals.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Standardise product codes, suppliers, lots, tests, methods and timing. Distinguish continuous data from attribute data; choose a chart suited to the sampling structure and frequency. Set the centre line and control limits from stable process data using an approved statistical procedure; do not confuse control limits with specification limits.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** When a point falls outside control or a run of trending points appears, open a root-cause investigation before adjusting the process. Do not remove a data point merely because it worsens a KPI. Keep the reason for exclusion, the approver and the original data. A dashboard must allow drill-down to the test report and the material lot.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Trend Analysis and Statistical Control\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Trend Analysis and Statistical Control</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Trend Analysis and Statistical Control</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Data</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Stratification</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Trend</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Action</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "43": {"title": "Reconciling Results Between Laboratories", "cat": "Reporting and Analysis", "body": ["When the buyer and the factory obtain different results, compare the method/version, sampling, conditioning, equipment, calibration, operator, reagents and assessment approach before arguing about quality.", "Carry out a round-robin or split-sample exercise when necessary, ensuring that the samples are homogeneous and the shipping conditions are suitable. A non-homogeneous sample can create a large discrepancy even when both laboratories work correctly.", "Agree in advance, in the contract or quality agreement, on the referee laboratory, the preferred method, the sampling rules and the cost of retesting.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** Results from two laboratories can be compared only when they measure the same property on sufficiently similar samples, and the method, version, specimen preparation, conditions, equipment and calculation are controlled. A difference does not necessarily prove that one laboratory is wrong; it may arise from sample non-uniformity, cutting position, specimen direction, the conditioning environment or handling.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Before sending samples for comparison, split the sample homogeneously or prepare parallel specimens according to the agreed plan. Seal and code the samples; send them with method instructions, a data form and reporting requirements. When the results arrive, build a comparison table for each sample and each parameter, not just two aggregated figures. Check for differences in method version, equipment, conditions and units.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** If the difference exceeds the level defined in the comparison plan, open an investigation in this order: sample homogeneity, procedural differences, equipment, operator competence, data handling. Do not simply average the two results to create a “correct” figure. The decision on which result to use must rest on the scope of competence, quality-control evidence and contract rules."], "deep": "Interlaboratory comparison requires homogeneous, coded samples and equivalent methods and conditions. Choose a proficiency testing or interlaboratory comparison programme suited to the test; evaluate the deviation, z-score or index as specified by the programme. If the difference is large, review sampling, conditioning, equipment, operator and calculation. Do not choose the most favourable result; identify the cause and the improvement action before the next comparison.\n\n**Technical analysis:** Results from two laboratories can be compared only when they measure the same property on sufficiently similar samples, and the method, version, specimen preparation, conditions, equipment and calculation are controlled. A difference does not necessarily prove that one laboratory is wrong; it may arise from sample non-uniformity, cutting position, specimen direction, the conditioning environment or handling.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Before sending samples for comparison, split the sample homogeneously or prepare parallel specimens according to the agreed plan. Seal and code the samples; send them with method instructions, a data form and reporting requirements. When the results arrive, build a comparison table for each sample and each parameter, not just two aggregated figures. Check for differences in method version, equipment, conditions and units.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** If the difference exceeds the level defined in the comparison plan, open an investigation in this order: sample homogeneity, procedural differences, equipment, operator competence, data handling. Do not simply average the two results to create a “correct” figure. The decision on which result to use must rest on the scope of competence, quality-control evidence and contract rules.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Reconciling Results Between Laboratories\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Reconciling Results Between Laboratories</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Reconciling Results Between Laboratories</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Split sample</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Comparison</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Analysis</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Deviation</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">CAPA</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "44": {"title": "ISO/IEC 17025 and Laboratory Competence Management", "cat": "Quality Management", "body": ["ISO/IEC 17025 sets requirements for the competence, impartiality and consistent operation of testing and calibration laboratories. Accreditation is specific to a given test and competence; it is not a stamp confirming that every test performed by the organisation is accredited.", "Controls cover the document system, personnel, equipment, metrological traceability, methods, sampling, sample handling, assurance of the validity of results and reporting. These activities must be supported by records.", "When using an external laboratory, check the scope of accreditation, method version, place of performance, subcontracting and suitability for the buyer's requirements. Do not look only at the accreditation logo on the report.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** ISO/IEC 17025 sets requirements for the competence, impartiality and consistent operation of testing and calibration laboratories. Applying it does not mean that every test in the laboratory automatically falls within the accredited scope. Distinguish between the internal quality management system, the actual technical competence and the accredited scope confirmed by the accreditation body.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Competence management starts with the scope of testing: method, sample matrix, measuring range, location, equipment and authorised personnel. Maintain records of method validation, estimation of measurement uncertainty where appropriate, metrological traceability, personnel competence, control of environmental conditions, handling of nonconforming work and evaluation of external providers. Document and record control must allow the whole testing process to be reconstructed.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** When presenting capability to a buyer, state only the tests and locations that fall within the current accredited scope; do not apply the accreditation symbol to results outside the scope where the rules do not allow it. Check the version of the standard and the accreditation body's requirements at the time of implementation; do not infer them from a summary."], "deep": "ISO/IEC 17025 sets requirements for the competence, impartiality and consistent operation of testing and calibration laboratories. The accredited scope must be checked by specific test, method, material and location; not every result issued by an accredited laboratory falls within the scope. Manage independence, confidentiality, personnel competence, equipment, methods, records and complaint handling. Maintain evidence of operation, not only records prepared for the assessment.\n\n**Technical analysis:** ISO/IEC 17025 sets requirements for the competence, impartiality and consistent operation of testing and calibration laboratories. Applying it does not mean that every test in the laboratory automatically falls within the accredited scope. Distinguish between the internal quality management system, the actual technical competence and the accredited scope confirmed by the accreditation body.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Competence management starts with the scope of testing: method, sample matrix, measuring range, location, equipment and authorised personnel. Maintain records of method validation, estimation of measurement uncertainty where appropriate, metrological traceability, personnel competence, control of environmental conditions, handling of nonconforming work and evaluation of external providers. Document and record control must allow the whole testing process to be reconstructed.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** When presenting capability to a buyer, state only the tests and locations that fall within the current accredited scope; do not apply the accreditation symbol to results outside the scope where the rules do not allow it. Check the version of the standard and the accreditation body's requirements at the time of implementation; do not infer them from a summary.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"ISO/IEC 17025 and Laboratory Competence Management\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>ISO/IEC 17025 and Laboratory Competence Management</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">ISO/IEC 17025 and Laboratory Competence Management</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Competence</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Scope</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Records</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Assessment</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "45": {"title": "PT, CRM and Internal Quality Control", "cat": "Quality Management", "body": ["Proficiency testing (PT) or interlaboratory comparison helps assess competence using test items with a reference value or a consensus result. A CRM is a reference material characterised with an accompanying value and uncertainty.", "For tests with no suitable PT programme, split samples, internal control samples, duplicate tests and trend monitoring can be used. Acceptance criteria and the action on deviation must be defined.", "Do not use PT results as a quality certificate for each shipment. PT assesses the laboratory's competence in a specific test and at a specific time.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** Proficiency testing (PT) and interlaboratory comparison provide evidence of the ability to perform a test relative to other organisations or to a reference value. A CRM (certified reference material) supports checking trueness/bias within the certified range of properties. They supplement internal quality control; they do not replace method verification and day-to-day monitoring.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Plan PT for the critical test scope; choose a programme with a suitable sample matrix and measuring range. For a CRM, check the certificate, the certified value, the uncertainty, storage conditions and expiry. Define in advance how results will be evaluated, who will review them and what action follows an unsatisfactory result. Use internal control samples to monitor performance between PT rounds.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** Do not use one good PT result to claim that every test in the laboratory is competent. When a PT result is abnormal, check the procedure, operator, equipment, sample, calculation and the suitability of the programme. Keep the result, the investigation, the action and the evidence of effectiveness."], "deep": "PT (proficiency testing) or interlaboratory comparison helps evaluate results against the group of participants; a CRM provides reference material with a certified value. Internal QC may use control samples, blanks, duplicates, spikes or control charts depending on the test. Define the frequency and alert criteria according to risk and method. When QC fails, stop releasing the related results, investigate and determine the range of results that may be affected.\n\n**Technical analysis:** Proficiency testing (PT) and interlaboratory comparison provide evidence of the ability to perform a test relative to other organisations or to a reference value. A CRM (certified reference material) supports checking trueness/bias within the certified range of properties. They supplement internal quality control; they do not replace method verification and day-to-day monitoring.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Plan PT for the critical test scope; choose a programme with a suitable sample matrix and measuring range. For a CRM, check the certificate, the certified value, the uncertainty, storage conditions and expiry. Define in advance how results will be evaluated, who will review them and what action follows an unsatisfactory result. Use internal control samples to monitor performance between PT rounds.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** Do not use one good PT result to claim that every test in the laboratory is competent. When a PT result is abnormal, check the procedure, operator, equipment, sample, calculation and the suitability of the programme. Keep the result, the investigation, the action and the evidence of effectiveness.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"PT, CRM and Internal Quality Control\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>PT, CRM and Internal Quality Control</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">PT, CRM and Internal Quality Control</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">PT/CRM</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Internal QC</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Control chart</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Fail handling</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "46": {"title": "Retained Sample Management and Chain of Custody", "cat": "Quality Management", "body": ["A retained sample needs an ID, description, lot, date of collection, collector, storage conditions, retention period and access rights. Chain of custody records each handover to ensure that the test sample is indeed the sample that was received.", "Storage conditions must avoid light, moisture, heat, dust, volatile chemicals and distortion, depending on the sample type. Some coloured samples or functional materials may change over time.", "When a complaint arises, compare the retained sample with the buyer's sample and the production sample, and check the storage history. A retained sample that has expired or whose identity is unclear should not be used as evidence for a conclusion.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** Retained samples support complaint investigation, verification of changes and later comparison of results. Chain of custody records who received, transferred, opened, divided, tested and stored the sample at each point in time. If the identity or condition of the sample is broken, later results may no longer prove which lot the sample belongs to.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Assign a unique ID; record the description, lot, colour, size, supplier, date received, seal condition and purpose of retention. Define which samples must be retained, the minimum quantity, temperature/humidity/light conditions, retention period and custodian. Each transfer must record the giver/receiver, time, quantity and seal condition. When subsamples are taken, link the sub-ID to the original sample.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** Do not store samples under conditions that change colour, moisture or chemistry and then use them for comparison as if they were in their original state. When the retention period expires, dispose of the sample according to the procedure and keep the disposal record. For samples used in a dispute, restrict access and record every opening."], "deep": "Chain of custody records who received, transferred, opened, divided, tested and stored the sample at each point in time. A retained sample needs a unique ID, storage conditions, retention period, location and authorised users. Seal it where necessary and record its condition on receipt, such as damaged packaging, moisture or a missing label. When a sample is divided, create sub-IDs linked to the original ID. Disposal of samples requires approval and records, especially for complaint or dispute samples.\n\n**Technical analysis:** Retained samples support complaint investigation, verification of changes and later comparison of results. Chain of custody records who received, transferred, opened, divided, tested and stored the sample at each point in time. If the identity or condition of the sample is broken, later results may no longer prove which lot the sample belongs to.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Assign a unique ID; record the description, lot, colour, size, supplier, date received, seal condition and purpose of retention. Define which samples must be retained, the minimum quantity, temperature/humidity/light conditions, retention period and custodian. Each transfer must record the giver/receiver, time, quantity and seal condition. When subsamples are taken, link the sub-ID to the original sample.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** Do not store samples under conditions that change colour, moisture or chemistry and then use them for comparison as if they were in their original state. When the retention period expires, dispose of the sample according to the procedure and keep the disposal record. For samples used in a dispute, restrict access and record every opening.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Retained Sample Management and Chain of Custody\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Retained Sample Management and Chain of Custody</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Retained Sample Management and Chain of Custody</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Receipt</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Handover</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Storage</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Disposal</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "47": {"title": "Developing Laboratory SOPs and Forms", "cat": "Quality Management", "body": ["An SOP should describe the purpose, scope, responsibilities, safety, equipment, reagents, sample conditions, each operating step, calculations, validity criteria and how to report.", "Forms should capture data at the time of performance: sample ID, operator, machine, date, conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations and approval. Avoid recording from memory after the test has finished.", "Control document versions, provide training when an SOP is revised and withdraw old copies from the point of use. When an external standard changes, assess the impact and validate the method before implementation.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** An SOP must enable a suitably competent person to perform the test consistently, while also indicating where professional judgement is required. The document needs the purpose, scope, responsibilities, definitions, equipment/chemicals, safety, sample preparation, sequence, calculations, quality control, deviation handling and records. Simply copying a standard method into an SOP without defining how it is carried out in the laboratory leaves gaps.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Link the SOP to the source method and version; define which steps may be adjusted and which may not. Forms must capture raw data, conditions, equipment, operator, samples, calculations, observations and approval. Trial the SOP with representative users, record points that are easily misunderstood and correct them before issue. Withdraw obsolete versions from the workplace.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** When a method changes, assess the impact on training, equipment, forms, historical data and competence. Do not allow an old printed copy to be used just because the new one is not yet at the test bench. Revision records must state the reason, the approver, the effective date and the content of the change."], "deep": "A laboratory SOP must state the purpose, scope, responsibilities, equipment, chemicals, environmental conditions, sequence, calculations, QC, safety and records. Forms must capture enough original data to reproduce the result, not only a conclusion box. Control the revision, effective date, author/approver and user training. When a standard changes, assess the impact on the SOP, forms, equipment and competence before issuing the new version.\n\n**Technical analysis:** An SOP must enable a suitably competent person to perform the test consistently, while also indicating where professional judgement is required. The document needs the purpose, scope, responsibilities, definitions, equipment/chemicals, safety, sample preparation, sequence, calculations, quality control, deviation handling and records. Simply copying a standard method into an SOP without defining how it is carried out in the laboratory leaves gaps.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Link the SOP to the source method and version; define which steps may be adjusted and which may not. Forms must capture raw data, conditions, equipment, operator, samples, calculations, observations and approval. Trial the SOP with representative users, record points that are easily misunderstood and correct them before issue. Withdraw obsolete versions from the workplace.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** When a method changes, assess the impact on training, equipment, forms, historical data and competence. Do not allow an old printed copy to be used just because the new one is not yet at the test bench. Revision records must state the reason, the approver, the effective date and the content of the change.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Developing Laboratory SOPs and Forms\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Developing Laboratory SOPs and Forms</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Developing Laboratory SOPs and Forms</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">SOP</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Forms</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Revision</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Training</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "48": {"title": "Laboratory Safety and Waste Management", "cat": "Quality Management", "body": ["The laboratory must assess risks from chemicals, heat, electricity, rotating machinery, glass, cutting knives and destructive test equipment. Provide eyewash, spill kits, fume extraction, PPE and escape routes according to the actual risk.", "Classify and store waste according to its nature; do not pour test solutions, dyes, solvents or metal-containing chemicals down the drain without permission. Labels and records must comply with local regulations.", "Train staff in handling spills, exposure, fire and equipment incidents. Periodically inspect safety equipment, ventilation and the condition of fire extinguishers.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** Risk assessment must start from the actual activities: cutting specimens, heating, pressing, abrasion testing, chemical testing, using solvents, operating moving equipment and handling samples with sharp edges. The same chemical can present different risks depending on concentration, quantity used, route of exposure and ventilation. The SDS is an important input document but does not replace a workplace assessment.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Build an inventory of chemicals, SDS, labels, storage locations, storage compatibility, protective equipment, spill-response resources and first-aid procedures. Classify waste by composition and hazard; do not pour test solutions, solvents or reagents down the drain unless the environmental procedure permits it. Label waste containers with the date generated, the person responsible and the receiving organisation.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** Periodically inspect escape routes, fume cupboards where the procedure requires them, fire extinguishers, spill kits, electrical cables and machine guarding. Training must be tied to specific tasks and repeated after an incident or when chemicals change. Do not mix waste on your own to “reduce the number of containers”; classification and disposal must follow the regulations applicable at the site and locally."], "deep": "Laboratory safety must be based on an inventory of actual hazards: chemicals, solvents, heat, pressure, cutting machines, tensile equipment and electricity. SDS, labels, ventilation, compatible storage and spill-response arrangements must be easily accessible at the workplace. Classify waste by its nature and by the receiving procedure; do not pour chemicals down the drain unless permitted. Record incidents, exposures, waste and periodic inspections.\n\n**Technical analysis:** Risk assessment must start from the actual activities: cutting specimens, heating, pressing, abrasion testing, chemical testing, using solvents, operating moving equipment and handling samples with sharp edges. The same chemical can present different risks depending on concentration, quantity used, route of exposure and ventilation. The SDS is an important input document but does not replace a workplace assessment.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Build an inventory of chemicals, SDS, labels, storage locations, storage compatibility, protective equipment, spill-response resources and first-aid procedures. Classify waste by composition and hazard; do not pour test solutions, solvents or reagents down the drain unless the environmental procedure permits it. Label waste containers with the date generated, the person responsible and the receiving organisation.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** Periodically inspect escape routes, fume cupboards where the procedure requires them, fire extinguishers, spill kits, electrical cables and machine guarding. Training must be tied to specific tasks and repeated after an incident or when chemicals change. Do not mix waste on your own to “reduce the number of containers”; classification and disposal must follow the regulations applicable at the site and locally.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Laboratory Safety and Waste Management\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Laboratory Safety and Waste Management</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Laboratory Safety and Waste Management</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Chemicals</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Equipment</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Waste</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Response</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "49": {"title": "From Laboratory Results to CAPA and Factory Improvement", "cat": "Applying Test Results", "body": ["A failing result must be converted into action with an owner, a deadline, the extent of affected lots and containment measures. Distinguish correction, which deals with the problem immediately, corrective action, which eliminates the cause, and preventive/risk action, which reduces the risk of recurrence.", "Link laboratory results to IQC, inline, endline, suppliers, recipes, machines and product versions. A repeatedly failing parameter may point to a systemic fault rather than an isolated incident.", "Close a CAPA when there is evidence of effectiveness: later lots pass, the trend improves and the cause is under control. Do not close it merely because retraining was carried out or one more form was issued.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** A laboratory result is a technical signal; CAPA begins when the factory identifies the problem, the extent of impact and the cause that must be eliminated. A failing result does not by itself indicate the cause. Samples must be linked to lot, process step, material, machine, shift, supplier and production conditions in order to find the cause-and-effect relationship.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Containment first: hold the related lots, identify goods already cut, sewn or shipped, test additional samples according to plan and notify at the right level. Next, carry out a root-cause analysis using shop-floor data; distinguish the direct cause, the systemic cause and contributing factors. Corrective action must be tied to the demonstrated cause, with an owner, a completion date and criteria for verifying effectiveness.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** Close the CAPA when there is evidence that the process has stabilised under representative conditions, not merely when the form has been signed. Check the next lots, supplier or sewing line concerned, according to the level of risk. Update the control plan, SOP, spec or training if the cause shows that the current system has not controlled the risk."], "deep": "Laboratory results must be converted into action with an owner: delimit the lots, assess the impact, find the cause, decide on disposition and verify effectiveness. Link test failures to the processes that may have caused them, such as materials, dyeing, finishing, cutting, sewing or washing. A CAPA needs evidence of the cause and repeat testing after the action. Close the CAPA when the data confirm that the problem has not recurred within the monitoring scope.\n\n**Technical analysis:** A laboratory result is a technical signal; CAPA begins when the factory identifies the problem, the extent of impact and the cause that must be eliminated. A failing result does not by itself indicate the cause. Samples must be linked to lot, process step, material, machine, shift, supplier and production conditions in order to find the cause-and-effect relationship.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Containment first: hold the related lots, identify goods already cut, sewn or shipped, test additional samples according to plan and notify at the right level. Next, carry out a root-cause analysis using shop-floor data; distinguish the direct cause, the systemic cause and contributing factors. Corrective action must be tied to the demonstrated cause, with an owner, a completion date and criteria for verifying effectiveness.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** Close the CAPA when there is evidence that the process has stabilised under representative conditions, not merely when the form has been signed. Check the next lots, supplier or sewing line concerned, according to the level of risk. Update the control plan, SOP, spec or training if the cause shows that the current system has not controlled the risk.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"From Laboratory Results to CAPA and Factory Improvement\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>From Laboratory Results to CAPA and Factory Improvement</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">From Laboratory Results to CAPA and Factory Improvement</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Failed result</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Containment</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Root cause</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">CAPA</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "50": {"title": "Designing a Test Programme for a New Style", "cat": "Applying Test Results", "body": ["Start from the tech pack, intended use, market, multi-layer construction, care labelling and risks. Draw up a list of incoming requirements for each material and the tests on the finished garment.", "Divide testing into gates: materials, lab dip/prototype, pilot, pre-production and bulk. Each gate has entry/exit criteria, an approver and rules for handling failures.", "Give priority to early testing of risks that are costly to fix, such as shrinkage, colour, coating delamination, seam strength and restricted chemicals. Keep the test plan matrix in the style file.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** A test programme is built from the product construction, intended use, target market, claims, materials, finishing processes and use risks. It does not start from the laboratory's equipment list. For a rain jacket, for example, the requirements for the fabric, seams, zips, taped seams and the finished product must be distinguished; a result on the fabric does not by itself prove that the whole jacket passes.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Break the spec and BOM down into the properties to be verified. For each property, record the test, method/version, specimens to be prepared, test stage, quantity, laboratory, criteria from a controlled document, timing and approver. Mark hold points: before bulk ordering, before cutting, after sample sewing or before shipment. Test early the risks that could change the design or the material source.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** When colour, fibre composition, supplier, coating or seam construction changes, reassess the change matrix. The plan must define the handling of failing results, retained samples and the final report. Close the programme only when every requirement has suitable evidence or a written deviation approval."], "deep": "A test programme for a new style starts from the brief, BOM, claims, market and care instructions. Build a risk matrix by material/colour/process step, and select tests at the development, pilot and bulk stages. Define the number of specimens, the source, method, criteria, lead time and approving party. Give priority to long-duration tests so that they do not block the production schedule. When the construction or supplier changes, reassess the plan.\n\n**Technical analysis:** A test programme is built from the product construction, intended use, target market, claims, materials, finishing processes and use risks. It does not start from the laboratory's equipment list. For a rain jacket, for example, the requirements for the fabric, seams, zips, taped seams and the finished product must be distinguished; a result on the fabric does not by itself prove that the whole jacket passes.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Break the spec and BOM down into the properties to be verified. For each property, record the test, method/version, specimens to be prepared, test stage, quantity, laboratory, criteria from a controlled document, timing and approver. Mark hold points: before bulk ordering, before cutting, after sample sewing or before shipment. Test early the risks that could change the design or the material source.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** When colour, fibre composition, supplier, coating or seam construction changes, reassess the change matrix. The plan must define the handling of failing results, retained samples and the final report. Close the programme only when every requirement has suitable evidence or a written deviation approval.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, operator, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not, by itself, create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Designing a Test Programme for a New Style\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Designing a Test Programme for a New Style</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Designing a Test Programme for a New Style</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Brief</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test matrix</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Timeline</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Bulk approval</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "51": {"title": "Setting Up a Laboratory Quality Dashboard", "cat": "Applying Test Results", "body": ["A dashboard should track samples received, turnaround time, on-time rate, first-pass yield, fail rate by test, number of retests, equipment status and overdue CAPA.", "Analyse by material group, supplier, buyer, style number and test type. Avoid ranking laboratories solely on sample volume or speed without considering complexity and reliability.", "Define the numerator, denominator, data period and exclusion rules clearly. A dashboard helps prioritise investigations; it does not replace review of the original report.", "**Technical basis and test scope.** A laboratory dashboard should reflect the ability to deliver reliable results on time, rather than simply counting completed tests. Useful groups of indicators include request volume, time from sample receipt to release, retest rate, rate of abnormal results, equipment status, sample backlog, outsourced work and the status of corrective actions.", "**Preparation, execution and required records.** Define each indicator by a formula and a data source. For example, turnaround time must specify the start point (valid sample receipt or receipt of the request) and the end point (report release or report approval). Separate the data by test group and priority level so that a fast test does not mask a backlog in a complex test. Assign each KPI an owner, an update frequency and an approved internal alert threshold.", "**Interpreting results and limits of conclusion.** Before data go onto the dashboard, check that sample ID, time, status and reason for retest are complete. A rising retest rate may stem from sample quality, method, equipment or handling; the dashboard only detects a signal and does not by itself prove the cause. Use trend charts and drill-down to the original records for investigation, and lock edit rights on released data."], "deep": "The dashboard should display sample status, backlog, result turnaround time, on-time rate, fail/retest rate, test group and causes of delay. Each KPI must have a definition, denominator, period, data source and owner. Distinguish the time from sample receipt to start of testing from the actual testing time. Do not use fail rate to rank suppliers where sample mix and risk level differ and have not been normalised.\n\n**Technical analysis:** A laboratory dashboard should reflect the ability to deliver reliable results on time, rather than simply counting completed tests. Useful groups of indicators include request volume, time from sample receipt to release, retest rate, rate of abnormal results, equipment status, sample backlog, outsourced work and the status of corrective actions.\n\n**Traceable procedure:** Define each indicator by a formula and a data source. For example, turnaround time must specify the start point (valid sample receipt or receipt of the request) and the end point (report release or report approval). Separate the data by test group and priority level so that a fast test does not mask a backlog in a complex test. Assign each KPI an owner, an update frequency and an approved internal alert threshold.\n\n**Investigation scenario:** Before data go onto the dashboard, check that sample ID, time, status and reason for retest are complete. A rising retest rate may stem from sample quality, method, equipment or handling; the dashboard only detects a signal and does not by itself prove the cause. Use trend charts and drill-down to the original records for investigation, and lock edit rights on released data.\n\nA complete record must link sample ID, lot, method and version, tester, equipment, environmental conditions, raw data, calculations, deviations, reviewer and final decision. Acceptance criteria must come from the specification, regulation or buyer document currently in force; a test method does not by itself create a pass/fail limit.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Setting Up a Laboratory Quality Dashboard\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Setting Up a Laboratory Quality Dashboard</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Setting Up a Laboratory Quality Dashboard</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Backlog</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">TAT</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Fail rate</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Dashboard</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "52": {"title": "Practical Case: Planning Tests for a Cotton T-Shirt", "cat": "Practice: Test Planning", "body": ["Simulated scenario: developing a cotton jersey T-shirt, sold in several markets, with a pigment print on the chest. The project team must define the tests before approving bulk. There is no fixed list of tests for every T-shirt; the plan must be based on claims, specification, market, construction and actual risk.", "Step 1 – define the test object: fabric code and composition, ground colour, ink/print code, seam construction, labels, trims, care/laundering procedure and sample lot. Separate fabric samples, print samples and finished garments, because each answers a different question.", "Step 2 – build the requirements matrix. Groups that may be considered include: GSM/width; dimensional stability and spirality; colourfastness to washing/rubbing; pilling; seam strength; appearance and print adhesion/appearance after care; restricted chemicals where applicable. The decision column must record the source of the requirement, method/version, number of specimens, pretreatment conditions, acceptance criteria and approver.", "Step 3 – classify the timing: development testing to compare materials; confirmation testing before bulk; incoming/periodic inspection; retesting when the supplier, dyeing/printing recipe or construction changes. Do not repeat every test at every stage unless the risk analysis and contract require it.", "Step 4 – confirm the samples: assign a unique ID to each sample and record photographs, sampling position, lot, revision, date received and the person handing over. Laboratory samples must represent the intended material and process; if the test sample differs from bulk, state clearly the limits of extrapolation.", "Exercise: the buyer sends the requirement “garment must retain appearance after home laundering” but does not specify the method or criteria. List the information that must be clarified before sending samples for testing; do not choose the number of cycles or the pass threshold yourself."], "deep": "The cotton T-shirt case requires the composition, colour, GSM, construction, finishing, care label and claims to be defined. Select samples from several rolls/lots if the order is variable; set up test groups for GSM, width, shrinkage, spirality, colourfastness to washing/rubbing/perspiration, and pH or chemicals as required. Separate development tests from bulk tests. Set the timeline, sample quantity and criteria from the specification; do not assign thresholds yourself. When a failure occurs, identify the lot and process stage concerned.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Practical Case: Planning Tests for a Cotton T-Shirt\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Practical Case: Planning Tests for a Cotton T-Shirt</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Practical Case: Planning Tests for a Cotton T-Shirt</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">T-shirt brief</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Sampling</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Test plan</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Decision</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "53": {"title": "Practical Case: Handling a Near-Limit Result and the Retest Decision", "cat": "Practice: Result Analysis", "body": ["Simulated scenario: a measurement result lies close to a limit in the specification. Before deciding pass/fail or requesting a retest, check the validity of the test, the decision rule and the measurement uncertainty established for the method/laboratory.", "Record check: is the sample ID and revision correct; has the sample been conditioned as required by the method; is the equipment within its calibration/verification validity; are the method and version correct; are there any deviations in handling, environment, accessories or raw data? A specimen must not be re-selected merely because the first result was unfavourable.", "If a technical error is found with evidence, record the nonconformity, assess its impact and carry out the retest under the approved procedure. If no error is found, do not delete the original result; any additional test must have a documented reason, plan, number of specimens and rule for combining results, all defined beforehand.", "Numerical example for learning only: the specification has a limit L set by the buyer; the laboratory reports a result x and an expanded uncertainty U. That x lies on the passing side of L does not automatically determine the conclusion if the decision rule takes U into account. The report must state the agreed rule rather than apply a general rule of its own.", "Exercise: a lot has a failing first result and a passing additional test. Write a record stating the reason for the additional test, how the original result was handled, how the specimens were selected and who has the authority to decide the lot status."], "deep": "A near-limit result requires review of the method, uncertainty, decision rule, sample variability and validity of the test run. Check the equipment/QC before concluding. If a retest is permitted, define beforehand the number of specimens, the sampling method and how the results will be combined; do not select only the favourable result. Record the original result, the reason for the retest, the subsequent result and the approver. If results fall on both sides of the limit, apply the agreed rule.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Practical Case: Handling a Near-Limit Result and the Retest Decision\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Practical Case: Handling a Near-Limit Result and the Retest Decision</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Practical Case: Handling a Near-Limit Result and the Retest Decision</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Near-limit</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">result</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Uncertainty</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Decision</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">rule</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Retest</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "54": {"title": "Practice: Sample Management, Chain of Custody and Test Records", "cat": "Practice: Laboratory Management", "body": ["A test sample may pass through several departments: merchandising submits the request, the warehouse receives the sample, the laboratory cuts specimens, the tester performs the test, QA reviews and an authorised person releases the report. If the link between sample and lot is lost, the result, however accurately measured, is not a sufficient basis for a decision.", "A sample identifier should link to the style number, material, colour, supplier/mill, lot, PO or project, revision, sampling position, date and time received, person delivering/receiving, packaging condition and tests requested. Do not use a file name or free-text description as the sole key.", "The chain-of-custody form records each handover, seal condition, quantity, storage location, time removed/returned and the responsible person. If a sample is wet, torn, contaminated or has lost its label, record the deviation before testing and request a decision on how to proceed.", "The test record comprises the request, method/version, environmental conditions, equipment and its status, person performing the test, raw data, calculations, photographs, deviations, technical review, decision and report version. Retained samples must have a retention period and storage conditions in accordance with the laboratory/buyer procedure.", "Exercise: two samples of the same colour but from different lots were placed in the same bag after cutting. Determine whether testing can continue, what must be quarantined and investigated, who has the authority to decide and which information fields must be recorded."], "deep": "Chain-of-custody practice requires a sample receipt ID, condition photographs, seals, date and time and the person handing over. Each time a sample is divided, create a sub-ID linked to the parent ID; record the quantity used, the quantity remaining and the storage location. When a sample shows signs of damage, wrong labelling or insufficient quantity, hold it and request confirmation before testing. The record must connect the test request, raw data, report, retained sample and disposal history.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Practice: Sample Management, Chain of Custody and Test Records\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Practice: Sample Management, Chain of Custody and Test Records</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Practice: Sample Management, Chain of Custody and Test Records</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Sample ID</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Chain of custody</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Records filed</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Traceability</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}, "55": {"title": "Practice: Laboratory Dashboard for Monitoring Progress and Result Quality", "cat": "Practice: Laboratory Data", "body": ["A dashboard is useful only when the indicator definitions, denominators, recording times and data sources are consistent. Do not mix the processing time of internal tests with buyer waiting time or sample transit time without distinguishing them.", "Indicators that can be designed include: requests received per period; requests completed; turnaround time by test group; on-time rate; number of amended reports; rate of tests invalidated by procedural error; number of instruments due for verification; number of overdue CAPA. Each indicator needs an owner and a calculation definition.", "Illustrative example: in one week, 80 requests are identified as eligible for TAT calculation; 68 requests are released within the committed time. On-time rate = 68/80 × 100% = 85%. Do not put the total of ineligible requests, or requests awaiting information, into the denominator if the KPI definition does not include them; report the number awaiting separately.", "Analyse by test type, customer, material group and cause of delay to find bottlenecks. Average TAT can be skewed by a few very long requests; consider showing the median, percentiles and distribution rather than a single average figure.", "Exercise: the dashboard shows TAT increasing during the month while the on-time rate is unchanged. State at least three possible explanations and the data that must be analysed before concluding that laboratory capability has declined."], "deep": "The laboratory dashboard should draw data from the sample-receipt log, machine schedule, results and released reports. Define consistent statuses such as received, awaiting preparation, in testing, awaiting review, released and on hold. Track backlog by test group and by equipment/staff capacity. Raise alerts for samples near their deadline, QC failures, retests and near-limit results. Check the quality of the input data before using the dashboard for management decisions.", "diagram": "<figure class=\"phase15-diagram\" style=\"margin:18px 0;padding:12px;border:1px solid #d7e0e7;border-radius:14px;background:#fff;overflow-x:auto\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 770 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Practice: Laboratory Dashboard for Monitoring Progress and Result Quality\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px;height:auto\"><title>Practice: Laboratory Dashboard for Monitoring Progress and Result Quality</title><text x=\"18\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#183247\">Practice: Laboratory Dashboard for Monitoring Progress and Result Quality</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"49\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#617586\">Testing workflow and data-control points</text><rect x=\"18\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Receipt log</text><path d=\"M184 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"212\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"294\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Status</text><path d=\"M378 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"406\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"488\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">KPI</text><path d=\"M572 110 h18 m-6 -6 l6 6 -6 6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b36b35\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><rect x=\"600\" y=\"76\" width=\"164\" height=\"68\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#edf3f8\" stroke=\"#4f718b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"682\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#183247\">Alerts</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#617586\">Trace sample ID, method, equipment, operator and result.</text></svg></figure>"}}, "categories": {"Hệ thống phòng lab": "Laboratory Systems", "Lấy mẫu & chuẩn bị": "Sampling and Specimen Preparation", "Quản lý thiết bị": "Equipment Management", "Thử nghiệm vật lý": "Physical Testing", "Thử nghiệm cơ lý": "Mechanical Testing", "Độ bền màu": "Colourfastness Testing", "Kích thước & hình dạng": "Dimensional Stability", "Tính năng chức năng": "Functional Performance", "Thử nghiệm sản phẩm": "Product Testing", "Đo màu & ngoại quan": "Colour Measurement and Appearance", "Hóa chất & tuân thủ": "Chemicals and Compliance", "Tính năng an toàn": "Safety Performance", "Báo cáo & phân tích": "Reporting and Analysis", "Quản trị chất lượng": "Quality Management", "Ứng dụng kết quả": "Applying Test Results", "Thực hành lập kế hoạch thử": "Practice: Test Planning", "Thực hành phân tích kết quả": "Practice: Result Analysis", "Thực hành quản trị phòng lab": "Practice: Laboratory Management", "Thực hành dữ liệu phòng lab": "Practice: Laboratory Data"}, "ui": {"eyebrow": "GARMENT ACADEMY · KNOWLEDGE LIBRARY", "pageTitle": "Textile Testing and Laboratory Management", "pageIntro": "Advanced learning on sampling, conditioning, physical and mechanical testing, colourfastness, functional performance, chemical testing, colour measurement, equipment management, result reliability and the use of laboratory data in garment manufacturing.", "flowTitle": "Test Control Workflow", "request": "Request", "specMethod": "specification / method", "sampling": "Sampling", "idConditioning": "ID / conditioning", "testing": "Testing", "equipmentRaw": "equipment / raw data", "evaluation": "Evaluation", "validityPass": "validity / pass-fail", "action": "Action", "releaseCapa": "release / CAPA", "abnormal": "Abnormal result → verify the specimen, method, equipment and operation before deciding whether to retest", "searchPlaceholder": "Search tests, equipment or technical terms…", "allCategories": "All content groups", "stats": "Filter by content group", "notFound": "No matching lesson was found", "tryAgain": "Try another keyword or select all content groups.", "technical": "Technical analysis and application", "warningTitle": "Professional limitation", "warningText": "This is training material, not a reproduction of ISO/AATCC/ASTM standards and does not grant access to copyrighted standards. Before commercial testing, use the current standard edition, suitable equipment, competent personnel and the laboratory's approved safety procedures.", "footerTagline": "A little of everything. All worth using.", "start": "GET STARTED", "home": "Home", "apps": "Apps", "tools": "Tools", "ai": "AI", "games": "Games", "academy": "ACADEMY", "dictionary": "English–Vietnamese–Chinese Dictionary", "supply": "Supply Chain and Trade", "digital": "Digital Transformation and Data Governance", "appnho": "APPNHO", "privacy": "Privacy Policy", "terms": "Terms of Use", "contact": "Contact", "about": "About AppNho", "footerNote": "Technical specifications must be checked against the tech pack, standards and approved requirements for each product.", "scopeTitle": "Laboratory Scope", "scopeHtml": "Phase 15 focuses on producing reliable test results: method requirements, sampling, conditioning, equipment, test operations, validity, reporting and laboratory competence management. Using test results for lot management, IQC/final-inspection decisions, NCR handling and CAPA belongs to <a href=\"Phase10_Quality_Management.html\">Phase 10 – Quality Management</a>; <a href=\"Phase13_Advanced_Materials.html\">Phase 13 – Material and Performance Validation</a>; and <a href=\"Phase5_Dyeing_Printing_Finishing.html\">Phase 5 – Dyeing, Printing and Finishing Process Control</a>. Each test must follow the approved standard revision, buyer method, specimen conditions and order requirements; acceptance limits must not be inferred from the test name."}};

